{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r1s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261003-002-r1s1\ntitle: \"Replace combinational division with a bounded multicycle execute unit\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline scores 12.12 iterations/s at a median 5.442 MHz. Its reported\n  worst path traverses forwarding and the execute ALU, with 156 cells and\n  199.837 ns arrival time. The ALU currently contains combinational signed\n  and unsigned division and remainder operators, making a fully unrolled\n  divider the leading explanation for this extreme depth. Such hardware\n  constrains the clock even when executing other instructions. The local\n  history contains only the baseline and has no prior structural attempts\n  or LESSONS.md. Removing this deep arithmetic path is more promising than\n  improving instruction throughput while keeping the same clock limit.\nhypothesis: |\n  Introduce one shared, blocking multicycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU unit in EX.\n  Use unsigned restoring division, computing eight quotient bits per cycle\n  with eight bounded subtract/select steps and registering the partial\n  remainder and quotient between groups. Four recurrence cycles replace\n  the current 32-step combinational operation. Capture forwarded operands\n  once, prepare magnitudes for signed operations, and register final sign\n  correction separately. Bound the schedule to eight EX service cycles:\n  capture, magnitude preparation, four recurrence cycles, sign correction,\n  and acceptance into EX/MEM. The existing liveness check triggers at\n  cycle 10 and requires the next retirement by cycle 20; this schedule\n  leaves room for pipeline bubbles, including a preceding taken branch.\n  Do not add extra handoff states or change any formal depth. Check the\n  bound on the complete pipeline as well as on the divider in isolation.\n  DIV and REM share quotient/remainder state; return the selected result\n  with RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics. Leave multiplication\n  and ordinary integer execution unchanged.\n\n  Hold fetch and ID/EX while the division is pending, but let older MEM/WB\n  instructions dr\n…[4781 of 7853 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:003f52e14c263afb]…\nrithmetic. Check bounded latency, result\n      stability under delayed acceptance, back-to-back operations and\n      reset during execution. Preserve existing arithmetic test coverage.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add division dependency chains into ALU/branch/load/store consumers,\n      back-to-back divisions, older stores and loads with repeated dmem\n      stalls, concurrent imem stalls, x0 destinations and reset while busy.\n      Include a taken branch directly followed by a divide and verify\n      retirement progress within the unchanged formal window on ready\n      buses, in both real and ALTOPS builds. Add div_unit.sv to this test's\n      existing explicit RTL source list so the pipeline DUT elaborates.\n      Assert captured RVFI operands/results, contiguous retirement orders,\n      no duplicate memory acceptance and no lost completion. Run existing\n      unit, cosim, formal and CoreMark gates unchanged, and measure the\n      vendor-timed circuit rather than RVFI-only logic.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
