{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'for p in cores/AGENTS.md cores/bench/AGENTS.md; do if [ -f \"$p\" ]; then cat \"$p\"; fi; done\ncat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (improvement, +465.59%): Statically removing combinational `/` and `%` from the core ALU and using an eight-cycle shared restoring DIV/REM unit shortened the arithmetic critical path enough for clock gains to outweigh added division stalls in CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (regression, +37.68%): Four radix-4 steps with registered D/2D/3D and parallel trial subtraction halved div_unit's serial subtract/select depth, raising median Gowin Fmax from 30.789 to 42.390 MHz at unchanged 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles, but a faster retimed divider won the round.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (improvement, +50.15%): Folding two narrow restoring steps into launch and moving sign correction to EX/MEM acceptance lets `div_unit` shorten serial subtract/select chains from eight steps to five within the same eight-edge service schedule, improving fitness without adding divider latency.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 (regression, +12.44%): Tagged next-PC caching reduced control-transfer bubbles and CoreMark cycles by 6.79%, but `div_unit` remained the critical path, leaving its 77.08 iter/s below divider retiming's 102.93 iter/s.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r3s2 (improvement, +11.19%): Deriving direct targets from the fetched instruction enables direction-only recovery with one predicted_taken bit, while raw-instruction decode with valid/ID/EX squashes removes the redirect-to-decode instruction-mask path, letting bimodal prediction improve CoreMark fitness alongside the retimed divider.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (improvement, +5.31%): A two-entry fall-through fetch queue lets instructions captured during load-use, dmem and divider holds feed decode when io_imemReady is low, improving stalled CoreMark throughput while preserving immediate decode through the empty-queue bypass.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, +3.15%): Deferring load-dependent branch checks to MEM with validity-only squashes cut CoreMark cycles by 2.51%, but div_unit and the register-file/ALU path still limited median Fmax to 48.605 MHz, leaving the fitness gain below the 4.6% acceptance threshold.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r6s0 (regression, +5.77%): Separating EX/MEM effective_addr from alu_result removes unused MUL logic from the BSRAM address cone, allowing fixed-latency radix-8/radix-4 divider lookahead to reach 51.124 MHz in the prototype at unchanged CoreMark cycles before alu_result becomes the next timing limit.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r6s1 (improvement, +18.24%): Combining registered 17-by-17 multiplier partials with radix-8/radix-4 division and registered odd multiples removes both M-extension timing bottlenecks while preserving a shallow two-trial divider launch, so clock gains outweigh CoreMark's small extra multiplier-stall cost.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r6s2 (regression, -2.70%): Moving load-dependent branch validation into EX with registered MEM recovery retained the 2.51% CoreMark cycle saving, but div_unit and forwarding/ALU-to-BSRAM paths remained critical at 45.847 MHz, so shortening the correction path failed to recover clock speed.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r7s1 (improvement, +21.78%): Combining banked flop register-file reads with ID/EX-registered dependency matches and parallel one-hot operand muxes shortens both storage and forwarding paths into the ALU, improving Fmax without adding a pipeline stage.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r7s2 (regression, -0.90%): Moving branch-prediction recovery from EX to registered EX/MEM added 1.64% CoreMark cycles but raised median Fmax only 0.73%, so shortening the redirect path did not pay for the extra recovery cycle.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
