pty-queue bypass.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, +3.15%): Deferring load-dependent branch checks to MEM with validity-only squashes cut CoreMark cycles by 2.51%, but div_unit and the register-file/ALU path still limited median Fmax to 48.605 MHz, leaving the fitness gain below the 4.6% acceptance threshold.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r6s0 (regression, +5.77%): Separating EX/MEM effective_addr from alu_result removes unused MUL logic from the BSRAM address cone, allowing fixed-latency radix-8/radix-4 divider lookahead to reach 51.124 MHz in the prototype at unchanged CoreMark cycles before alu_result becomes the next timing limit.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r6s1 (improvement, +18.24%): Combining registered 17-by-17 multiplier partials with radix-8/radix-4 division and registered odd multiples removes both M-extension timing bottlenecks while preserving a shallow two-trial divider launch, so clock gains outweigh CoreMark's small extra multiplier-stall cost.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r6s2 (regression, -2.70%): Moving load-dependent branch validation into EX with registered MEM recovery retained the 2.51% CoreMark cycle saving, but div_unit and forwarding/ALU-to-BSRAM paths remained critical at 45.847 MHz, so shortening the correction path failed to recover clock speed.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r7s1 (improvement, +21.78%): Combining banked flop register-file reads with ID/EX-registered dependency matches and parallel one-hot operand muxes shortens both storage and forwarding paths into the ALU, improving Fmax without adding a pipeline stage.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r7s2 (regression, -0.90%): Moving branch-prediction recovery from EX to registered EX/MEM added 1.64% CoreMark cycles but raised median Fmax only 0.73%, so shortening the redirect path did not pay for the extra recovery cycle.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r8s2 (improvement, +5.42%): Predicting four-way forwarding sources in ID/EX, including separate WB ALU and load data, shortens EX's arithmetic path by removing late reg_write qualification, priority logic and WB's mem_to_reg mux, while separate canceled-write replay preserves correctness.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
