{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r6s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261003-001-r6s0\ntitle: \\\"Isolate memory address generation to unlock fixed-latency divider lookahead\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 120.53 iterations/s at 48.333 MHz and 4,010,178\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Previous divider lookahead exposed the\n  register-file/ALU path as the next clock limit: r5s0 reached only\n  49.850 MHz and gained 3.14 percent. EX/MEM alu_result currently serves\n  both arithmetic forwarding/writeback and the data-memory address.\n  Vendor timing consequently includes multiplication on a path ending\n  at a data-memory BSRAM address input, although MUL never requests\n  memory. Separating that address cone should remove this ceiling on\n  the fixed-latency divider optimization.\nhypothesis: |\n  Decouple load/store effective-address generation from the general\n  ALU result, enabling radix-8/radix-4 divider lookahead to improve Fmax\n  without immediately hitting the ALU-to-memory-address timing limit.\n  This is one coordinated micro-optimization of combinational arithmetic\n  paths and their endpoint mapping. Keep every pipeline stage, service\n  schedule, predictor, queue, forwarding priority and bus handshake.\n\n  Add a 32-bit effective_addr field to the existing EX/MEM bundle.\n  On the same advancing edge as the normal EX payload, capture the\n  full-width sum of forwarded rs1 and in.imm. Use a dedicated addition\n  expression independent of ALU opcode, operand-B selection, arithmetic\n  result selection and jump-link selection. Apply the existing EX/MEM\n  reset, bubble and dmem-hold rules. No extra stage or cycle is added.\n  Computing this sum for non-memory instructions is harmless because\n  their memory enables remain deasserted.\n\n  Use effective_addr in MEM for the bus address, byte offsets, store\n  masks, load extraction, alignment checks and RVFI aligned memory\n  address. Continue copying in.alu_result into MEM/WB; retain that\n  original result for register writeback and EX/MEM forwarding. Never\n  substitute an effective address for an arithmetic register value.\n  Preserve full 32-bit addition without address-range assumptions or\n  truncation, misaligned-access traps and suppression of memory enables.\n\n  Pair this address-cone isolation with the prior fixed-latency\n  radix-8/radix-4 divider recurrence. Retain the two narrow launch\n  steps and all six recurrence states. On launch, capture unsigned\n  35-bit 3D, 5D and 7D from the NEW divisor magnitude using independent\n  widened expressions D+(D<<1), D+(D<<2), and (D<<3)-D. Derive 2D,\n  4D and 6D by wiring/shifts. Widen before shifting, avoid general\n  constant multipliers, and never initialize from the old divisor.\n\n  Each recurrence first forms the 35-bit trial T={R,Q[31:29]}.\n  Subtract kD for k=1..7 in parallel using explicit 36-bit unsigned\n  differences. With fit[k] equal to inverse borrow, form exclusive\n  enables e0=\"'!fit[1], ek=fit[k]&&!fit[k+1] for k=1..6, e7=fit[7].\n  Select T[31:0] for digit zero or the selected difference[31:0]\n  through a masked-OR mux, then append the three-bit quotient digit.\n  Follow with a radix-4 step using a 34-bit trial, exact 34-bit\n  D/2D/3D and 35-bit differences, selecting two more quotient bits\n  identically. Register the final quotient/remainder in the existing\n  registers. Six groups still consume 30 bits after the two-bit\n  prologue, preserving corrected acceptance on service edge eight.\n\n  For nonzero divisors, R<D ensures selected remainders fit 32 bits.\n  Preserve original operands, signed magnitudes, quotient/remainder\n  sign correction, zero-divisor overrides and INT_MIN/-1 results.\n  Keep DONE stable under arbitrary backpressure, reset cancellation\n  and once-only acceptance. Keep exact ALTOPS formulas and the same\n  handshake schedule, compiling real arithmetic out under ALTOPS.\n  Preserve the multiplier, decoder, nret=1 IO, RVFI order, formal\n  depths and all existing correctness checks.\n\n  Validate the real sequential divider, every load/store width and\n  alignment, addresses dependent on forwarded arithmetic results,\n  held requests and alternating M/ALU/memory traffic. Run unchanged\n  formal and RVFI cosimulation gates and canonical stalled CoreMark\n  CRC/UART/marker checks. Expected cycles remain 4,010,178. Acceptance\n  requires fitness above 126.07438 iterations/s, about 50.56 MHz at\n  that count. Additional address flops, trial-subtractor routing and\n  the remaining arithmetic-to-writeback path are physical risks.\n\n  An isolated scratch RTL prototype of these exact changes used the\n  unchanged vendor flow, wrapper, constraints and stall generator.\n  Placement options 0/1/2 achieved 50.412/51.124/51.124 MHz, median\n  51.124 MHz. Option-0 area was 3,807 LUT4 and 1,019 FF, six DSPs,\n  six BSRAMs and eight LUT-RAMs. Its worst reported path ends at\n  EX/MEM alu_result instead of the memory BSRAM address. At unchanged\n  cycles this implies 127.48561 iterations/s, a 5.77086 percent gain.\n  This is prototype evidence, not official tournament acceptance;\n  the margin above the 4.6 percent threshold is modest. Implementation\n  must rerun timing and all correctness gates. Only this hypothesis\n  file is delivered; prototype files remain outside repository RTL.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a full-width effective_addr field to ex_mem_t, separate\n      from alu_result. Keep existing fields and top-level interfaces.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture forwarded rs1 plus immediate in EX/MEM effective_addr\n      on the existing advancing edge, with unchanged hold, bubble\n      and reset behavior. Preserve arithmetic results and forwarding.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use effective_addr for dmem_addr, alignment, lane masks, load\n      extraction and RVFI aligned memory address. Propagate the\n      original alu_result for register writeback and retain memory\n      enable gating, trap behavior and held WB forwarding payloads.\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace five serial restoring steps per recurrence with\n      parallel-trial radix-8 then radix-4 selection, using registered\n      widened odd divisor multiples and exclusive masked-OR muxes.\n      Preserve eight service edges, prologue, signs, exceptions,\n      ALTOPS, reset cancellation and result acceptance protocol.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing arithmetic, latency and progress assertions.\n      Add quotient-digit thresholds kD-1/kD/kD+1, wide unsigned\n      multiples, group boundaries and randomized reference checks.\n      Exercise held DONE and reset in real and ALTOPS builds.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all checks; exercise load/store widths, misalignment\n      and addresses dependent on forwarded ALU, MUL and DIV results,\n      including dmem holds and alternating non-memory operations.\n      Check byte masks, data, RVFI addresses and once-only effects.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r6s0.yaml'\nd=yaml.safe_load(open(p));jsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261003-001-r6s0' and d['category']=='micro_opt'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r6s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261003-001-r6s0\ntitle: \\\"Isolate memory address generation to unlock fixed-latency divider lookahead\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 120.53 iterations/s at 48.333 MHz and 4,010,178\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Previous divider lookahead exposed the\n  register-file/ALU path as the next clock limit: r5s0 reached only\n  49.850 MHz and gained 3.14 percent. EX/MEM alu_result currently serves\n  both arithmetic forwarding/writeback and the data-memory address.\n  Vendor timing consequently includes multiplication on a path ending\n  at a data-memory BSRAM address input, although MUL never requests\n  memory. Separating that address cone should remove this ceiling on\n  the fixed-latency divider optimization.\nhypothesis: |\n  Decouple load/store effective-address generation from the general\n  ALU result, enabling radix-8/radix-4 divider lookahead to improve Fmax\n  without immediately hitting the ALU-to-memory-address timing limit.\n  This is one coordinated micro-optimization of combinational arithmetic\n  paths and their endpoint mapping. Keep every pipeline stage, service\n  schedule, predictor, queue, forwarding priority and bus handshake.\n\n  Add a 32-bit effective_addr field to the existing EX/MEM bundle.\n  On the same advancing edge as the normal EX payload, capture the\n  full-width sum of forwarded rs1 and in.imm. Use a dedicated addition\n  expression independent of ALU opcode, operand-B selection, arithmetic\n  result selection and jump-link selection. Apply the existing EX/MEM\n  reset, bubble and dmem-hold rules. No extra stage or cycle is added.\n  Computing this sum for non-memory instructions is harmless because\n  their memory enables remain deasserted.\n\n  Use effective_addr in MEM for the bus address, byte offsets, store\n  masks, load extraction, alignment checks and RVFI aligned memory\n  address. Continue copying in.alu_result into MEM/WB; retain that\n  original result for register writeback and EX/MEM forwarding. Never\n  substitute an effective address for an arithmetic register value.\n  Preserve full 32-bit addition without address-range assumptions or\n  truncation, misaligned-access traps and suppression of memory enables.\n\n  Pair this address-cone isolation with the prior fixed-latency\n  radix-8/radix-4 divider recurrence. Retain the two narrow launch\n  steps and all six recurrence states. On launch, capture unsigned\n  35-bit 3D, 5D and 7D from the NEW divisor magnitude using independent\n  widened expressions D+(D<<1), D+(D<<2), and (D<<3)-D. Derive 2D,\n  4D and 6D by wiring/shifts. Widen before shifting, avoid general\n  constant multipliers, and never initialize from the old divisor.\n\n  Each recurrence first forms the 35-bit trial T={R,Q[31:29]}.\n  Subtract kD for k=1..7 in parallel using explicit 36-bit unsigned\n  differences. With fit[k] equal to inverse borrow, form exclusive\n  enables e0=\"'!fit[1], ek=fit[k]&&!fit[k+1] for k=1..6, e7=fit[7].\n  Select T[31:0] for digit zero or the selected difference[31:0]\n  through a masked-OR mux, then append the three-bit quotient digit.\n  Follow with a radix-4 step using a 34-bit trial, exact 34-bit\n  D/2D/3D and 35-bit differences, selecting two more quotient bits\n  identically. Register the final quotient/remainder in the existing\n  registers. Six groups still consume 30 bits after the two-bit\n  prologue, preserving corrected acceptance on service edge eight.\n\n  For nonzero divisors, R<D ensures selected remainders fit 32 bits.\n  Preserve original operands, signed magnitudes, quotient/remainder\n  sign correction, zero-divisor overrides and INT_MIN/-1 results.\n  Keep DONE stable under arbitrary backpressure, reset cancellation\n  and once-only acceptance. Keep exact ALTOPS formulas and the same\n  handshake schedule, compiling real arithmetic out under ALTOPS.\n  Preserve the multiplier, decoder, nret=1 IO, RVFI order, formal\n  depths and all existing correctness checks.\n\n  Validate the real sequential divider, every load/store width and\n  alignment, addresses dependent on forwarded arithmetic results,\n  held requests and alternating M/ALU/memory traffic. Run unchanged\n  formal and RVFI cosimulation gates and canonical stalled CoreMark\n  CRC/UART/marker checks. Expected cycles remain 4,010,178. Acceptance\n  requires fitness above 126.07438 iterations/s, about 50.56 MHz at\n  that count. Additional address flops, trial-subtractor routing and\n  the remaining arithmetic-to-writeback path are physical risks.\n\n  An isolated scratch RTL prototype of these exact changes used the\n  unchanged vendor flow, wrapper, constraints and stall generator.\n  Placement options 0/1/2 achieved 50.412/51.124/51.124 MHz, median\n  51.124 MHz. Option-0 area was 3,807 LUT4 and 1,019 FF, six DSPs,\n  six BSRAMs and eight LUT-RAMs. Its worst reported path ends at\n  EX/MEM alu_result instead of the memory BSRAM address. At unchanged\n  cycles this implies 127.48561 iterations/s, a 5.77086 percent gain.\n  This is prototype evidence, not official tournament acceptance;\n  the margin above the 4.6 percent threshold is modest. Implementation\n  must rerun timing and all correctness gates. Only this hypothesis\n  file is delivered; prototype files remain outside repository RTL.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a full-width effective_addr field to ex_mem_t, separate\n      from alu_result. Keep existing fields and top-level interfaces.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture forwarded rs1 plus immediate in EX/MEM effective_addr\n      on the existing advancing edge, with unchanged hold, bubble\n      and reset behavior. Preserve arithmetic results and forwarding.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use effective_addr for dmem_addr, alignment, lane masks, load\n      extraction and RVFI aligned memory address. Propagate the\n      original alu_result for register writeback and retain memory\n      enable gating, trap behavior and held WB forwarding payloads.\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace five serial restoring steps per recurrence with\n      parallel-trial radix-8 then radix-4 selection, using registered\n      widened odd divisor multiples and exclusive masked-OR muxes.\n      Preserve eight service edges, prologue, signs, exceptions,\n      ALTOPS, reset cancellation and result acceptance protocol.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing arithmetic, latency and progress assertions.\n      Add quotient-digit thresholds kD-1/kD/kD+1, wide unsigned\n      multiples, group boundaries and randomized reference checks.\n      Exercise held DONE and reset in real and ALTOPS builds.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all checks; exercise load/store widths, misalignment\n      and addresses dependent on forwarded ALU, MUL and DIV results,\n      including dmem holds and alternating non-memory operations.\n      Check byte masks, data, RVFI addresses and once-only effects.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r6s0.yaml'\nd=yaml.safe_load(open(p));jsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261003-001-r6s0' and d['category']=='micro_opt'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Validated cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r6s0.yaml\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
