{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(s)\n if d.get('category')=='micro_opt':\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'])\n  print({k:d.get(k) for k in ('outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','critical_path')})\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n baseline-bench-e192068 Baseline retest for cores/bench\n{'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 12.12, 'fmax_mhz': 5.442, 'cycles': 4491485, 'critical_path': {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -189.542, 'arrival_ns': 199.837, 'logic_cells': 156, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}}\n\n\n hyp-20261003-001-r1s0 Shared combinational divider with fused subtract and compare\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 10.42, 'fmax_mhz': 4.682, 'cycles': 4491485, 'critical_path': {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -223.572, 'arrival_ns': 233.867, 'logic_cells': 156, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex']}}\nReplace only the real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapath with one explicitly\nshared, combinational unsigned quotient-and-remainder array, surrounded\nby RV32M sign conversion and exceptional-result selection. Keep the ALU\ninterface, zero-cycle combinational latency, pipeline registers, stalls,\nforwarding, multiplier, and all non-division operations unchanged. This\nis an arithmetic circuit optimization, with no iterative execution unit\nor additional pipeline stage.\n\nUse 32 statically elaborated restoring steps, from quotient bit 31 down\nto bit 0. Start R at the unsigned dividend magnitude. At quotient bit k,\nset W=32-k and subtract divisor[W-1:0] from R[31:k] using an explicitly\nunsigned W+1-bit subtraction. Accept the subtraction only when its\nborrow bit is clear and all divisor bits above W-1 are zero; the latter\nguard is constant true at W=32. Set quotient[k] to that decision and,\nwhen accepted, replace R[31:k] with the low W difference bits while\npreserving R[k-1:0]. Use elaboration-time special cases for empty slices.\nAfter the last step R is the remainder. This computes quotient and\nremainder together, narrows early steps, and uses the subtraction's own\nborrow instead of a separate relational comparator followed by another\nsubtraction. Do not calculate the remainder through a serial q*divisor\n\n…[25226 of 28298 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:866cfce4edd32369]…\nnged\nCoreMark evaluator accepted canonical CRCs, UART validation text,\ncompletion, in-bounds execution and timing markers, reporting exactly\n4,010,178 cycles for ten iterations with both bus stall streams.\nTheir product is 127.48561 iterations/s, a 5.77086 percent gain.\n\nThe final prototype passed all 27 existing pipeline cocotb tests\nin both real and ALTOPS builds. Selftest and all three random\nprograms passed full RVFI comparison against the unchanged ISS\nwith both instruction and data bus stalls. The same divider\ndatapath separately passed its three existing unit tests in both\nreal and ALTOPS builds, including exact latency, held results and\nreset cancellation. Formal proofs were not rerun for this proposal\nand remain required implementation gates. This is prototype evidence,\nnot official tournament acceptance; the margin above the 4.6 percent\nthreshold is modest. Implementation must rerun timing and correctness\ngates. Only this hypothesis file is delivered; prototype files remain\noutside repository RTL.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
