{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_91", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r8s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261003-001-r8s0\ntitle: \"Specialize registered results to shorten memory and forwarding feedback\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 173.56 iterations/s at 70.074 MHz and 4,037,538\n  CoreMark bracket cycles. A fresh vendor option-0 run reproduced\n  63.674 MHz and the logged 21.0 ns path through forwarding, the ordinary\n  ALU and EX result selection into data-memory BSRAM. Other prototypes\n  expose the WB load/ALU mux on the same feedback path. The r7s1 winner\n  removed register-read and dependency-comparison limits, but address\n  generation and forwarding still traverse result selection unnecessary\n  for their individual consumers. Prior r6s0 address isolation and\n  r7s0 mux flattening each left another path limiting the clock.\nhypothesis: |\n  Specialize the registered values delivered to memory and forwarding,\n  removing general-purpose result muxes from their feedback paths.\n  Combine a dedicated effective-address register, exclusive parallel\n  EX result selection, and load/ALU writeback selection before MEM/WB.\n  These are one coordinated combinational result-routing optimization:\n  preserve every stage boundary, instruction latency, forwarding priority,\n  predictor, fetch queue, M-unit schedule and bus handshake.\n\n  Add a full-width effective_addr field to the existing EX/MEM bundle.\n  Capture forwarded raw rs1 + in.imm on the same edge as the other EX\n  payload. This sum must be independent of ALU opcode, ALU operand-B\n  substitution, jump-link selection and MUL/DIV result selection.\n  Use the existing reset, bubble and dmem-hold rules. Do not truncate\n  addresses or assume an executable/data region from benchmark behavior.\n\n  In MEM use effective_addr for dmem_addr, byte offsets and masks,\n  load extraction, alignment traps and aligned RVFI memory addresses.\n  Leave general EX/MEM alu_result available for EX forwarding and\n  architectural result propagation; an address must never substitute\n  for a non-memory arithmetic producer's result. Memory enables and\n  misaligned-access \n…[3607 of 6679 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6e02f127f055d850]…\nisting EX/MEM edge. Replace the nested DIV/MUL/link/ALU\n      result mux with the specified exclusive masked-OR selection.\n      Preserve all reset, hold, bubble, M-unit and trap behavior.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use effective_addr consistently for memory addressing, lanes,\n      alignment checks and RVFI. Preselect extended load versus ALU\n      writeback data before the existing MEM/WB register. Preserve\n      retained WB payloads under hold_wb and all side-effect gating.\n  - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive w_data directly from the preselected MEM/WB result, removing\n      the late mem_to_reg mux. Keep write validity and destination rules.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing tests and add directed load/store lane, signed\n      load, offset/carry and alignment cases with immediate EX/WB\n      consumers. Check retained-WB forwarding through dmem/M holds,\n      exact RVFI results and once-only memory effects in both modes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
