{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport shutil\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/561ff4c6/00236225/micro_compare');d=p/'local_bht'/'rtl'\nshutil.copytree('cores/bench/rtl',d,dirs_exist_ok=True)\nf=d/'if_stage.sv';s=f.read_text();start=s.index('  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\\\\n    if (reset) begin\\\\n      for (int i = 0; i < 64; i++) counters[i]');end=s.index('\\\\n  assign imem_addr = pc;',start)\ns=s[:start]+'''  generate for (genvar i=0; i<64; i++) begin : g_counter\n    wire h = counters[i][1];\n    wire l = counters[i][0];\n    always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n      if (reset) counters[i] <= 2'b01;\n      else if (branch_train && branch_index == 6'(i)) begin\n        counters[i][1] <= (h && l) || (branch_outcome && (h || l));\n        counters[i][0] <= (h && \"'!l) || (branch_outcome && (h || !l));\n      end\n    end\n  end endgenerate\n'\"'''+s[end:];f.write_text(s)\nshutil.copytree('cores/bench/test',p/'local_bht/test',dirs_exist_ok=True)\nPY\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nfrom tools.eval import gowin\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/561ff4c6/00236225/micro_compare')\nr=gowin.build_all(Path.cwd(),p/'local_bht/rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',p/'local_bht/timing',options=(0,1,2))\n(p/'local_bht.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2)); print(json.dumps(r))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r9s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-001-r9s0',\n'title':'Local saturating-counter updates remove shared predictor feedback',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 182.96 iterations/s at 73.869 MHz and 4,037,538 CoreMark bracket cycles. The latest timing path crosses EX recovery and frontend control; local scratch variants also expose EX-to-direction-counter paths. In if_stage, a variable-index read, saturating arithmetic and variable-index write describe the training update. Expressing each counter as independently enabled local state can remove the shared old-counter selection and arithmetic from this feedback network. The r7s2 recovery retiming lost throughput, and r8s0 result specialization did not improve the accepted score. This proposal changes only counter circuit topology, with identical prediction and training behavior.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Implement the existing 64 two-bit bimodal counters as 64 constant-index generated register words, each with its own training enable and explicit Boolean saturating transition. Preserve the same table size, indexing, state encoding, initialization, lookup and training edge. This is a micro-optimization of the existing state-update circuit, not a predictor-policy change, a new predictor or delayed training.\n\nFor entry i, use enable_i = branch_train && (branch_index == i), comparing at exactly six bits. Let h=counters[i][1], l=counters[i][0] and t=branch_outcome. On an enabled update, assign next_h = (h && l) || (t && (h || l)); next_l = (h && \"'!l) || (t && (h || !l)). Each next-state bit is a three-input Boolean function of this entry'\"'s old state and outcome. Use elaboration-time constant indexing so there is no counters[branch_index] old-state read in the update datapath. Reset each entry synchronously to 01 with priority over training; otherwise hold every nonselected entry. Keep the dynamic counters[pc[7:2]][1] fetch lookup unchanged.\n\nThese equations implement taken transitions 00->01, 01->10, 10->11, 11->11 and not-taken transitions 00->00, 01->00, 10->01, 11->10. Preserve nonblocking assignment semantics: fetch and ID/EX must observe the pre-edge prediction on simultaneous same-index training. Every update uses the current entry state at resolution, never the counter value sampled when the instruction was fetched. Do not add write-through, reset scrubbing, forwarding of a newly trained direction, or an extra training register.\n\nKeep branch_train qualification, alias behavior, direct-target derivation and its full-width bounds/alignment checks exactly as today. Preserve the two-entry queue, fall-through bypass, redirect priority, stalls, raw decode, forwarding/replay, ALU and M units. Maintain nret=1, all public IO, exact traps, monotonic retirement, memory side-effect suppression, arithmetic correctness and symbolic progress. No evaluation or contract files change.\n\nThe intended benefit is replacing a global read/modify/write selection network with local LUT-sized next-state functions and decoded enables. This can shorten training feedback and reduce routing pressure without changing cycles. Gowin may already implement part of this transformation, and outcome/enable fanout or the divider and ALU paths may cap the gain; the estimate therefore has low confidence.\n\nValidate all eight old-state/outcome combinations, both saturation endpoints, reset versus simultaneous training, every table index, alias collisions, back-to-back updates and simultaneous fetch/training of the same entry. Check prediction sequences and accepted tokens against the original counter model with independent instruction/data stalls and redirects. Preserve existing tests and run unchanged formal, RVFI cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks. At identical inputs and stall streams, expect exactly 4,037,538 bracket cycles. Judge median vendor Gowin Fmax times measured iterations/cycle. Acceptance requires more than 191.37616 iterations/s, approximately 77.268 MHz at unchanged cycles; a speculative five-percent forecast is not a claim that this threshold has been measured.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':5,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Replace only the dynamic-index saturating-counter update process with 64 generated, constant-index two-bit register updates. Use the local Boolean next-state equations, six-bit index enable, reset-to-01 priority and nonselected-entry holds specified above. Preserve the fetch read expression and simultaneous read/training semantics, all prediction eligibility and targets, queue control and PC updates.'''}, {'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'''Keep every existing check. Add an independent two-bit counter reference covering all eight transitions, saturation, all 64 indices, aliasing and repeated updates. Check pre-edge predictions on simultaneous same-index fetch/training, reset priority, and token predictions retained across queue holds and redirects. Do not relax the existing handshake or timing expectations.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Wrote and schema-validated',p)\nfor h in range(2):\n for l in range(2):\n  for t in range(2):\n   n=(((h and l) or (t and (h or l)))<<1) | ((h and not l) or (t and (h or not l)))\n   assert n==(min(3,2*h+l+1) if t else max(0,2*h+l-1))\nprint('All eight counter transitions match')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
