{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/561ff4c6/11309482/mulmem')\nr=build_all(Path.cwd(),p/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',p/'timing')\n(p/'timing_result.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2))\nprint(json.dumps(summarize(r),indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"placement_failed\": false,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 74.34,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    73.602,\n    74.34,\n    74.34\n  ],\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      73.602,\n      18\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      74.34,\n      15\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      74.34,\n      15\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"lut4\": 5345,\n  \"ff\": 1935,\n  \"lutram\": 0,\n  \"bsram\": 4,\n  \"dsp\": 2,\n  \"logic_levels\": 18,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_div/quotient_30_s1\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_13_s1\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -8.587,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 18.881,\n    \"logic_cells\": 70,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_div\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_33", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r11s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r11s1',\n 'title':'Pipeline multiply completion through MEM with load-style dependencies',\n 'category':'structural',\n 'motivation':'''The champion executes every multiply as a blocking two-edge EX operation, although its four partial products are already registered. Completion then passes through the general EX result mux and EX/MEM. Retiming the same arithmetic across EX and MEM can remove that global execution hold, remove the multiplier completion input from the EX/address-result cone, and let independent instructions follow a multiply. Recent divider and branch-recovery changes have exposed competing ALU/address and branch-feedback timing limits, so a change to the existing execution-stage ownership is more promising than another branch-comparator rewrite.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Turn multiplication into a two-stage pipelined execution path using the existing EX and MEM boundaries. EX captures the four signed 17-by-17 partial products on the same edge that the MUL instruction enters EX/MEM. In the following cycle the existing carry-save recombination produces the architectural product and MEM/WB captures the selected low or high half. The instruction retires through the unchanged single WB boundary. Independent younger instructions may enter EX immediately; only a true dependent consumer needs the existing one-cycle load-style interlock. Keep division's blocking eight-service-edge schedule and all ordinary instruction stage boundaries unchanged.\n\nAllow the multiplier to accept a new operation on the same edge its previous result is accepted by MEM. Register partial products, signedness/result selection and ALTOPS operands as one token; the pre-edge old result belongs to the older MEM instruction, while new partials belong to the younger EX instruction after the edge. An unaccepted result remains stable. The core launches only on valid, legal, advancing EX work with verified forwarding sources and no older dmem hold. Match launch exactly to EX/MEM insertion; wrong-path, replayed, reset and held instructions must not launch. Reset cancels pending work. Preserve the standalone multiplier's current blocking handshake through a default-off pipelining parameter, or an equivalent separate internal pipeline implementation, so existing unit assertions are not weakened.\n\nAssociate multiplier completion with the corresponding valid legal MUL/MULH/MULHSU/MULHU in EX/MEM. Accept it exactly when that instruction transfers to MEM/WB, never from the younger ID/EX instruction's opcode. There is at most one multiply result awaiting MEM because dispatch and EX/MEM are in order. If MEM cannot advance, retain both its instruction token and multiplier partials. An older stalled load/store prevents the multiply from launching; once the multiply reaches MEM it is not itself a dmem transaction. Consecutive independent multiplies can exchange old completion and new launch on every edge without overwriting either result.\n\nRoute the completed product into the existing MEM/WB read_data result bank, selecting it instead of load extraction only for the valid multiply in MEM. Give the multiply token mem_to_reg=1 at EX/MEM so existing MEM/WB load-bank forwarding and WB result selection deliver its product. mem_read and mem_write remain zero: this is an internal result-kind encoding, not a bus read. Retain the actual instruction, source operands and RVFI metadata. A multiply reports zero memory masks/address as before. Its EX/MEM alu_result must not contain the multiplier completion or be used as its architectural product; with the core's combinational multiply disabled, this field may be the ordinary zero ALU output for M opcodes. Remove the MUL result arm from the EX/MEM general-result mux.\n\nExtend the existing producer-not-ready interlock to a legal multiply in ID/EX, using its nonzero rd and the same conservative raw source-field comparisons currently used for loads. Keep load and multiply dependency handling in one stage-movement rule: hold the queue head and insert one ID/EX bubble unless older memory/divider holds take priority. On the following eligible edge the consumer captures a WB-load-bank source prediction for that multiply via EX/MEM.mem_to_reg. No valid consumer may select EX/MEM.alu_result for a not-yet-complete multiply. Preserve youngest-writer priority, x0 behavior, pending result holds and retained WB forwarding after retirement-valid clears. Do not add a combinational multiplier-result-to-EX bypass; the benefit requires retaining the product's MEM/WB boundary.\n\nMUL operands are now consumed and its RVFI source values recorded on the launch/EX-advance edge, so use the current validated forwarded rs1/rs2 for that token. Keep saved operand snapshots for the still-blocking divider. Remove multiply busy/valid from execute_wait and from the exemption that suppresses operand-permission replay while the current instruction owns a blocking unit: a pending multiply now belongs to the older MEM instruction and must not hide a younger consumer's canceled-source failure. Preserve replay precedence, exact branch outcomes/training, all alignment traps and all decoder legality rules. The multiply unit's ALTOPS branch must use the same new pipeline acceptance timing as the real arithmetic, with unchanged exact substitute formulas.\n\nForecast seven percent higher fitness with low confidence. The opportunity is chiefly higher Fmax from removing multiply wait/completion from EX control and result paths, with a smaller possible cycle reduction from independent multiplies. Immediate dependent multiplies still pay one issue bubble, and memory result selection or the divider may become the next bottleneck. No measured gain is claimed until the prototype or implementation passes the unchanged checks and median Gowin timing. Acceptance requires actual fitness above 191.37616 iterations/s, using the measured bracket cycle count rather than assuming every old multiply hold disappears.\n\nValidate independent back-to-back multiplies, all signed/high-half variants, same-edge old-result acceptance and new launch, dependency chains into ALU/branch/load/store/MUL/DIV, multiple writers to the same register, x0, held older memory operations, simultaneous bus stalls, reset and canceled-source replay. Preserve the standalone arithmetic tests and add explicit pipelined-mode handshake tests. Run all existing real/ALTOPS pipeline and arithmetic checks, unchanged formal and stalled RVFI cosimulation, and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker/bounds validation. Preserve all existing progress bounds, nret=1 and external ports. This is one retiming of multiplication across existing stages; do not change predictor policy, divider recurrence, memory protocol or evaluation files.''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/mul_unit.sv','description':'Add a default-off pipelined handshake mode that accepts a new launch simultaneously with acceptance of the old registered partial products. Preserve existing blocking mode, exact real/ALTOPS arithmetic, signed limb widths, result stability and reset cancellation. All current standalone assertions remain valid in default mode.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Launch multiplication on the ordinary EX advance, recording current validated forwarded operands in EX/MEM. Remove MUL from execute_wait and from the blocking-unit replay exemption. Remove multiply completion from EX/MEM.alu_result selection; mark its result kind for MEM/WB read_data. Expose the registered-partial completion to MEM and take acceptance from the older MEM token. Keep blocking division, replay, branch and trap behavior unchanged.'},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Capture the associated multiply product into MEM/WB.read_data when the valid MUL token advances, while keeping actual load extraction for loads. Preserve all RVFI source metadata, normal WB forwarding retention, zero multiply memory masks, and dmem enables derived only from true memory operations.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire multiply result and acceptance between EX-owned partial-product storage and the MEM token. Extend the existing load-use not-ready qualification to valid legal ID/EX multiplies with nonzero destinations. Use EX/MEM multiply identity to select the MEM result and accept completion, independent of younger opcodes; preserve nret and top-level IO.'},\n {'file':'test/test_mul_unit.py','description':'Keep all existing blocking-mode arithmetic and busy-start assertions. Add a separate pipelined-mode runner covering consecutive accepted launches, simultaneous old completion/new capture, holds, reset, all four multiply variants, exact real/ALTOPS outputs and token ordering.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Add independent and dependent multiply streams, mixed MUL/load result forwarding, writer collisions, x0, consecutive high-half variants, MEM holds, MUL-to-DIV/branch/store dependencies and canceled-source replay while an older multiply completes. Preserve all current architectural and bounded-progress assertions; check once-only ordered retirement and no multiply memory requests.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Replaced and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
