{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_39", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r12s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261003-002-r12s1\ntitle: \\\"Use replicated distributed-RAM register storage with reset-valid tags\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's banked flop register file removed the old inferred-BSRAM\n  read bottleneck in r7s1, but it stores 992 resettable data bits and reads\n  them through two wide selection trees. The core now uses 5,310 LUT4 and\n  1,929 registers. Preserve r7/r8's registered operand and four-source\n  forwarding boundaries while replacing only register storage with small\n  replicated asynchronous LUT RAMs. Separate reset validity from data so\n  RAM inference does not reintroduce either data-reset flops or synchronous\n  BSRAM reads. This structural storage alternative has not been evaluated\n  in the logged structural attempts.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the 31 independently resettable flop words in reg_file with\n  two identical storage replicas, one for each combinational read port.\n  Partition each replica into two 16-word by 32-bit banks selected by\n  architectural address bit 4. Both replicas receive each accepted\n  nonzero-register write, with the low four address bits selecting a\n  word and the high bit enabling exactly one bank. Read both banks of\n  the selected replica asynchronously, then select the required bank.\n  Use inference-friendly unreset arrays and Gowin's distributed RAM\n  attribute, and verify that the vendor implementation actually uses\n  RAM16 resources instead of BSRAM or a large flop/mux implementation.\n  Keep the synthesizable behavioral arrays as the functional source for\n  simulation and formal; do not substitute an unrelated verification model.\n\n  Add 31 resettable written-valid bits, one per architectural register\n  x1..x31. Synchronous reset clears only those bits. It also suppresses\n  data-array writes on the reset edge. A permitted write sets its valid\n  bit and updates both storage copies atomically. Reads of an invalid\n  register return zero regardless of stale or uninitialized RAM payload.\n  A second reset must hide all previously written payload without a\n  scrub loop, added reset latency or assumption about FPGA RAM power-up.\n  x0 remains a constant zero and cannot acquire a valid bit or a write.\n\n  Preserve the existing write-first combinational bypass exactly:\n  w_en && w_addr \"'!= 0 && w_addr == rs_addr selects w_data, ahead of\n  stored-data validity, independently on both ports. This includes a\n  same-cycle write to a previously invalid register and simultaneous\n  dual-port reads of the write destination. Keep reset behavior aligned\n  with the existing interface, whose combinational bypass is not gated\n  by reset even though the reset edge suppresses architectural writes.\n  Mask invalid data with a conditional selection that guarantees a\n  defined zero; never expose an uninitialized RAM word through RVFI.\n\n  Keep both asynchronous read ports and the existing ID/EX operand\n  capture edge. No extra pipeline stage, synchronous read conversion,\n  multi-pumping, clock-domain change, altered WB timing, or new hazard\n  is permitted. Retain the current four-way predicted forwarding masks,\n  EX/MEM versus WB priority, separate registered WB ALU/load values,\n  canceled-write replay, retained forwarding during dmem holds and\n  captured operands during blocking M execution. The storage change\n  must be architecturally and cycle-for-cycle transparent.\n\n  This is one structural register-storage hypothesis. Leave the ALU,\n  divider recurrence, multiplier schedule, branch predictor, fetch queue,\n  load-use policy and bus handshake unchanged. Preserve nret=1, all core\n  ports, decoder legality, trap discipline, memory bounds, contiguous\n  RVFI retirement, all arithmetic and bounded-progress assertions, and\n  canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/MMIO-bracket checks. Do not change any\n  protected evaluation file or use synthesis-only architectural behavior.\n\n  The opportunity is lower storage/read-selection area and shorter\n  local operand reads, with less routing pressure around EX forwarding\n  and the divider. Unlike the original BSRAM-backed register file, these\n  shallow LUT RAM banks have asynchronous reads before the existing\n  ID/EX boundary. Inspect mapped RAM type and the retained boundary;\n  fewer RTL registers alone is not evidence of a better implementation.\n  RAM placement, valid-bit selection and the write-first mux may instead\n  hurt timing, and the unchanged divider/branch paths may cap the gain.\n\n  Forecast six percent fitness improvement with low confidence. Expect\n  exactly 4,037,538 bracket cycles for ten iterations under the existing\n  stall streams. Acceptance requires more than 191.37616 iterations/s,\n  approximately 77.27 MHz at that cycle count; the forecast is not a\n  measured score. Judge the complete vendor placement median and actual\n  CoreMark throughput. Run existing register-file tests, full real and\n  ALTOPS pipeline suites, unchanged formal and RVFI cosimulation gates,\n  and canonical stalled CoreMark validation on the final implementation.\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace resettable flop-word storage and masked-OR read trees with\n      two replicated asynchronous register memories, each split into two\n      16x32 banks. Use unreset data arrays suitable for distributed RAM,\n      31 synchronous reset-valid bits, atomic writes to both replicas,\n      defined zero reads for invalid/x0 words and exact write-first bypass.\n      Preserve the module interface and ID/EX read-capture latency. Check\n      Gowin maps storage to RAM16 and does not absorb reads into BSRAM.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all existing assertions. Add exhaustive register-pair and\n      bank-boundary reads, first-write bypass on both ports, randomized\n      replica-coherence checks, repeated resets after populated storage,\n      reset/write collisions, x0 write rejection and invalid-word zero\n      reads. Check outputs immediately after reset and before first write\n      without assuming RAM data initialization or allowing a scrub delay.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add dependency sequences spanning both register banks, WB-to-ID\n      write-first collisions on both sources, nonadjacent reads after\n      writers drain, reset of previously populated architectural state,\n      retained-WB forwarding during long data stalls and M operand capture.\n      Keep exact architectural results, RVFI sources/order/PC, all original\n      stall and retirement-gap assertions, and real/ALTOPS schedules.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r12s1.yaml')\ns=p.read_text().replace('changes:\\\\n','expected_impact:\\\\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\\\\n  confidence: low\\\\nchanges:\\\\n',1)\np.write_text(s)\nd=yaml.safe_load(s);jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()));print('VALID',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
