{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (improvement, +465.59%): Statically removing combinational `/` and `%` from the core ALU and using an eight-cycle shared restoring DIV/REM unit shortened the arithmetic critical path enough for clock gains to outweigh added division stalls in CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (regression, +37.68%): Four radix-4 steps with registered D/2D/3D and parallel trial subtraction halved div_unit's serial subtract/select depth, raising median Gowin Fmax from 30.789 to 42.390 MHz at unchanged 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles, but a faster retimed divider won the round.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (improvement, +50.15%): Folding two narrow restoring steps into launch and moving sign correction to EX/MEM acceptance lets `div_unit` shorten serial subtract/select chains from eight steps to five within the same eight-edge service schedule, improving fitness without adding divider latency.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 (regression, +12.44%): Tagged next-PC caching reduced control-transfer bubbles and CoreMark cycles by 6.79%, but `div_unit` remained the critical path, leaving its 77.08 iter/s below divider retiming's 102.93 iter/s.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r3s2 (improvement, +11.19%): Deriving direct targets from the fetched instruction enables direction-only recovery with one predicted_taken bit, while raw-instruction decode with valid/ID/EX squashes removes the redirect-to-decode instruction-mask path, letting bimodal prediction improve CoreMark fitness alongside the retimed divider.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (improvement, +5.31%): A two-entry fall-through fetch queue lets instructions captured during load-use, dmem and divider holds feed decode when io_imemReady is low, improving stalled CoreMark throughput while preserving immediate decode through the empty-queue bypass.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, +3.15%): Deferring load-dependent branch checks to MEM with validity-only squashes cut CoreMark cycles by 2.51%, but div_unit and the register-fil\n…[2123 of 5195 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f19dfcb0e04d0a4e]…\nto-fetch feedback and div_unit remained timing limits, so the median Fmax drop from 73.869 to 73.497 MHz erased the cycle benefit.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r12s1 (regression, -6.00%): Replicated asynchronous RAM with reset-valid tags saved prototype area without reducing CoreMark cycles, so improving throughput required recovering placed Fmax, which even parallel bank/validity/write-first bypass lanes failed to achieve.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r12s2 (regression, -7.91%): Address-region prediction left CoreMark cycles unchanged, yet median Fmax fell from 73.869 to 68.030 MHz with forwarding/branch/fetch control still critical, so shortening the memory-address adder alone did not remove the timing bottleneck.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r13s2 (regression, -7.92%): Snapshot training with unreset banked bit planes mapped the BHT to two RAM16SDP2 primitives, but forwarding/EX-to-ID feedback remained critical and median Fmax fell from 73.869 to 67.967 MHz, overwhelming the 0.08% CoreMark cycle saving.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
