to\": \"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_30_s0\",\n      \"slack_ns\": -8.528,\n      \"arrival_ns\": 18.822,\n      \"logic_cells\": 13,\n      \"modules\": [\n        \"clock_ibuf\",\n        \"cpu/u_ex\",\n        \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\n        \"cpu/u_ex\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_67", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r14s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r14s1',\n'title':'Register execute recovery with same-cycle target fetch',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 182.96 iterations/s at 73.869 MHz and 4,037,538\nCoreMark bracket cycles. Its reported path starts at EX/MEM.alu_result\nand traverses branch recovery to forwarding-register control. Registered\nrecovery previously failed in r7s2: it added 1.64 percent of cycles while\nregister-file/ALU timing limited clock improvement. The later register\nand forwarding changes removed those old bottlenecks. Revisit the control\npipeline boundary, but bypass the registered correction target directly\ninto fetch so the extra recovery register need not cost a ready-bus cycle.\nThis differs from adding fetch capacity, changing prediction policy or\nmerely delaying recovery with another pipeline bubble.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Introduce a one-cycle registered EX event boundary carrying correction\nvalid, the complete 32-bit correction target, branch-training valid,\ntraining index and actual outcome. Resolve operands, branches, target\nalignment and canceled-write replay in EX exactly as today, but capture\nthe event rather than driving IF/ID/forwarding control from the current\ncomparison. Capture only advancing, valid, verified execution; canceled\nsource replay independently captures its consumer PC without admitting\nthat consumer to EX/MEM. Registers reset invalid, each event is delivered\nonce, and a recovery cycle cannot generate events from the killed younger\nEX instruction. Register training as well as correction so the comparator\nno longer drives the direction table's write network combinationally.\n\nIn the following recovery cycle, use the saved target as the instruction\nbus address immediately: fetch_pc = recovery_valid ? recovery_target : pc.\nThe two-entry queue's wrong-path head is unavailable in this cycle; select\nthe target bus token directly, with availability determined by imemReady.\nIts PC, branch-table index, immediate-derived target, prediction and PC+4\nall use fetch_pc. If the target fetch is accepted, let that token enter\nID/EX on the same edge that kills the younger wrong-path EX instruction.\nAdvance the stored fetch PC to that target token's predicted successor.\nIf the bus is unready, latch recovery_target into PC, clear ID/EX to an\ninvalid zero-control bubble and fetch it later. Never increment past an\nunaccepted target. Reset takes priority over all of this.\n\nFlush every old queue token on recovery, but do not discard a newly\naccepted target token. If accepted and consumed directly, leave occupancy\nzero; if accepted but not consumed, retain exactly that complete token as\nthe sole queue entry. Outside recovery preserve the current fall-through\nqueue, conservative full refusal and fetch acceptance independent of\ndecode readiness. The recovery address comes only from registered state,\nnot from the current EX comparison. Retain full-width target arithmetic,\nalignment checks and executable-range prediction checks.\n\nSeparate killing the old EX token from admitting the new target into ID.\nRecovery overrides hazards and execute_wait belonging to the discarded\nyounger instruction; it must not flush the newly accepted target. Clear\nEX/MEM controls and valid for the killed token, suppress its M launch,\ncompletion acceptance, branch training, correction and memory effects,\nand let the older resolving EX/MEM instruction advance normally to WB.\nDo not hold the resolving branch for an extra retirement-service cycle.\nA pending recovery must correspond to a memory-free resolving branch/jump\nor to a replay whose EX/MEM entry is a bubble; assert this invariant so\nrecovery cannot discard an unrelated stalled memory transaction.\n\nFor target-token forwarding capture, exclude the killed current ID/EX\ninstruction from EX-producer write intent. The older EX/MEM instruction\nadvances to WB on this edge, so retain the existing MEM-permission-based\nWB source prediction, including a legal JAL/JALR link value. Preserve\nwrite-first RF bypass, youngest-writer priority, separate WB ALU/load\nbanks, retained WB forwarding under dmem holds and independent canceled-\nwrite verification. Replay has priority over a branch correction when\nforming an event. A killed M instruction must never start even if its\nbusy/result flags would otherwise request a hold.\n\nKeep the 64-counter predictor policy and instruction-derived direct\ntargets. Training now applies once from the registered event, one cycle\nlater, using the table's current counter and normal pre-edge read/update\nsemantics. This timing can slightly change alias behavior and measured\ncycles; it is not a new predictor. Keep ordinary integer/M execution,\nload-use interlocking, M service schedules, nret=1, all public ports,\ndecoder legality, trap/link rules, memory bounds and ordered RVFI intact.\nWith ready buses, correction-target decode occurs on the same edge as in\nthe champion: the target-address bypass compensates for the event register.\nThe existing branch-to-divider and formal liveness bounds remain required.\n\nThis is a single structural retiming of recovery from EX through fetch\nand target admission. The target bypass is essential to avoid the cycle\npenalty of the earlier registered-recovery attempt. Its costs are a new\nregistered-source address mux and recovery-aware token admission; the\nunchanged divider or ordinary ALU may still limit Fmax. Forecast six percent\nfitness improvement with low confidence. Acceptance requires more than\n191.37616 iterations/s, about 77.27 MHz if bracket cycles stay near 4,037,538.\nUse actual stalled cycles and the full prescribed Gowin placement median.\n\nRun unchanged formal, real/ALTOPS arithmetic checks, RVFI cosimulation and\ncanonical CoreMark CRC/UART/MMIO-bracket validation. Update internal\ncycle-reference models to describe registered event delivery and direct\ntarget admission with exact cycle equality, not relaxed timing bounds.\nPreserve every architectural, once-only-side-effect and progress assertion.\nCover all branch predicates, both prediction directions, target-fetch\nready/unready transitions, full-queue recovery, replay, misaligned jumps,\nlink forwarding, target loads/stores/M operations, wrong-path M launch\nsuppression, same-index training and reset around event delivery. Deliver\nonly this hypothesis; implementation remains a separate phase.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Register correction/replay and training events at the EX boundary.\nAdd an internal recovery-kill input that suppresses the younger token's\nlaunches, completions, events and EX/MEM admission while the older token\ndrains. Keep exact branch, target, trap and operand-replay calculations,\nall arithmetic and M schedules. Events are resettable and delivered once.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Drive fetch from the registered recovery target during event delivery.\nBuild and predict that complete target token with its actual fetch PC,\nbypass wrong-path stored tokens, and admit it immediately when ready.\nInvalidate the old queue, retain at most one unconsumed accepted target,\nand advance PC only on acceptance; otherwise hold the recovery address.\nApply registered training once with current-counter saturation.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'''Distinguish registered recovery from a normal load-use flush. Recovery\nkills old EX work while permitting ready target decode, disregarding\nhazards and M waits of the killed instruction. Preserve normal memory\nbackpressure, load-use behavior and zero-control invalid capture. Do not\nadd a WB/EX-MEM recovery-service hold or relax any progress requirement.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire the registered recovery event into EX kill, IF target selection\nand target-admission control. Disable current ID/EX write intent for\nforwarding capture on recovery, preserving older EX/MEM-to-WB permission.\nKeep nret=1 RVFI and every external port unchanged. Assert the recovery\nownership invariant and suppression of younger effects.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'''Adapt the exact fetch model to recovery-target bypass and acceptance.\nTest full and empty queues, target-ready/unready transitions, direct\ntarget branches/JAL predictions, same-index delayed training, reset,\ncomplete-token retention and no duplicated or skipped target fetch.\nPreserve all address, prediction, handshake and sequence assertions.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_hazard_unit.py','description':'''Check recovery's separate old-EX kill and target-ID acceptance semantics,\nincluding apparent load-use/M holds from killed work and unready target\nfetch. Retain all normal dmem/load-use/execute-wait and bubble checks.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Use an exact registered-event/reference schedule while retaining every\nISA, RVFI, side-effect and bounded-progress check. Cover correction and\ncanceled-write replay with full queues, dmem/imem stalls, target link\nconsumers, wrong-path stores and M operations, misalignment and reset.\nAssert no launched killed M operation, no repeated event or retirement,\nand unchanged ready-bus branch-to-divider progress. Verify both real\nand ALTOPS execution without changing their formulas or service times.\n'''}]}\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef represent(dumper, value):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str', value, style='|' if '\\\\n' in value else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,represent)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=88))\nprint('Overwrote',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
