t cases;',len(pairs)*4,'6+6*4+2 division/remainder cases')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"PASS: 49083 signedness/product cases; 65444 6+6*4+2 division/remainder cases\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_36", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r5s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\ntitle: \\\"Balance the M-extension execution pipeline with registered partial arithmetic\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 120.53 iterations/s at 48.333 MHz and 4,010,178\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md and the structural r1s1/r2s1\n  experiments show that registering long arithmetic paths is effective.\n  Current timing alternates between two limits: five serial restoring\n  steps in div_unit and the register-file/forwarding/ALU path that still\n  contains combinational multiplication. The r4s0 divider-only change\n  reached 50.635 MHz with its worst path moving to the ALU. Removing both\n  long M-operation cones through balanced execution registers offers more\n  clock headroom than changing only the current worst path.\n\n  An architectural replay of the unchanged canonical 2K, ten-iteration,\n  -O3 binary counted 93,960 MUL instructions and no DIV/REM instructions\n  between markers, and reproduced the five expected CRC values. Thus one\n  additional service cycle per multiply has a nominal cost of only 2.34\n  percent of current cycles. This is trace evidence, not candidate timing\n  or a stalled pipeline simulation.\nhypothesis: |\n  Repartition M-extension execution around registered partial arithmetic.\n  Give multiplication a two-service-edge execution path and rebalance\n  division within its existing eight-service-edge path. Ordinary integer\n  instructions retain their current single-cycle EX path. This is one\n  structural register-placement and scheduling change to the M backend:\n  both existing arithmetic bottlenecks must be bounded for a substantial\n  clock gain. Keep single issue, the current stage topology, fetch queue,\n  predictor, load-use discipline and retirement width.\n\n  Remove all real multiplication logic statically from the core's fast ALU,\n  just as combinational division is already excluded. Keep default ALU\n  parameters compatible with standalone tests. Add a blocking mul_unit\n  with start, busy, result_valid, result and result_accept. At the first\n  service edge, capture four 16-bit-limb partial products directly from\n  the forwarded operands, plus operation/sign-selection and RVFI source\n  snapshots. At the second service edge, EX/MEM accepts their recombined\n  result. There must be a real register boundary between limb products\n  and recombination; registering the completed original 32x32 product\n  leaves its long forwarding-to-multiply path intact.\n\n  One exact decomposition uses signed 17-bit limbs. Low limbs are\n  {1'b0,a[15:0]} and {1'b0,b[15:0]}. High limbs are\n  {signed_a && a[31],a[31:16]} and {signed_b && b[31],b[31:16]}.\n  Set signed_a for MULH/MULHSU and signed_b for MULH; MUL/MULHU can\n  use unsigned limbs. Register the four signed 34-bit products LL, LH,\n  HL and HH, which should fit the FPGA's small DSP multipliers. Recombine\n  P = LL + (LH << 16) + (HL << 16) + (HH << 32), modulo 2\"'^64, with\n  explicit sign extension to 64 bits before shifting. Select P[31:0]\n  for MUL and P[63:32] for the three high-product operations. Avoid a\n  serial chain of three wide carry-propagating additions: compress the\n  four aligned terms with two carry-save layers and one final carry\n  propagation, exploiting constant low/high bits. No new sign-correction\n  cycle is needed. Preserve full architectural operand widths and do not\n  specialize multiplication to CoreMark operand values.\n\n  result_valid becomes true after the partial-product capture edge; the\n  result is then a combinational function solely of frozen partials and\n  captured operation. EX/MEM accepts it on service edge two if not held.\n  Keep partials, operation and captured source values stable for arbitrarily\n  delayed acceptance. Reset cancels pending work; busy starts cannot\n  overwrite it. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, return the exact existing four\n  substitute formulas over captured original operands through the same\n  two-edge handshake and wait behavior. Test the sequential unit directly;\n  the legacy combinational ALU tests alone cannot validate this new path.\n\n  Repartition div_unit'\"'s existing 2 + 6*5 restoring steps into\n  6 + 6*4 + 2 steps, without changing its eight service edges. Launch\n  captures forwarded operands, magnitudes and signs while consuming the\n  first six dividend bits. Implement those six prologue trials at their\n  actual widths of one through six bits: subtract only the low W divisor\n  bits using W+1 bits and accept only when the borrow is clear and all\n  divisor bits above W-1 are zero. Share upper-bit-zero reductions. This\n  avoids putting six full-width subtractors after magnitude conversion.\n  Then each of the six existing recurrence edges consumes four bits with\n  the existing unsigned 33-bit subtract/borrow semantics, registering the\n  quotient/remainder after each group. Those edges finish 30 total bits.\n\n  In DONE, perform the final two restoring trials combinationally from\n  the frozen registered partial state, then apply quotient/remainder\n  selection, sign correction and architectural exceptional results. The\n  existing EX/MEM acceptance edge captures the fully completed result on\n  service edge eight. result_valid still first asserts after edge seven;\n  it denotes the final two-step-plus-correction result, not the unfinished\n  30-bit state. This balances launch, recurrence and acceptance logic while\n  reducing the repeated full-width chain from five steps to four. Do not\n  insert PREP, SIGN or handoff states. Preserve division by zero, INT_MIN/-1,\n  signed truncation/remainder semantics, exact ALTOPS formulas and the same\n  control schedule for real and ALTOPS builds. DONE sources remain frozen\n  under backpressure, so the combinational final result remains stable.\n\n  Generalize EX's existing blocking-divider control to either M unit.\n  A valid legal instruction owns ID/EX until its selected result is accepted.\n  Capture post-forward source values at launch before older producers drain.\n  During M wait, allow older MEM/WB instructions to drain, inserting a\n  zero-control EX/MEM bubble unless an older dmem hold requires retaining\n  EX/MEM. Dmem backpressure retains highest priority. Forward an accepted\n  M result through the normal EX/MEM and MEM/WB paths; never advertise an\n  unfinished product as a register value. Preserve retained WB forwarding\n  across holds, once-only stores/retirement, redirect and predictor-training\n  qualification, reset cancellation, traps and contiguous RVFI order.\n  The existing fetch queue may continue filling during execution waits.\n  Preserve nret=1 and every top-level memory/RVFI port.\n\n  Forecast a 12 percent fitness improvement with medium confidence. A\n  simple non-overlap estimate adds 93,960 multiply cycles, giving 4,104,138\n  cycles. At that count, approximately 55.40 MHz would deliver the forecast;\n  acceptance above 126.07438 iterations/s requires more than 51.743 MHz.\n  Actual stalled cycles must be measured: fetch overlap and changed bus\n  phases can alter this estimate. Principal risks are DSP inference, the\n  wider launch prologue, the two-step divider completion plus negation,\n  multiplier recombination, and a new non-M critical path. Check vendor\n  timing for the mapped partial-product registers and all three divider\n  register boundaries; arithmetic step counts alone do not establish Fmax.\n\n  A Python model checked the signed-limb/carry-save product decomposition\n  on 49,083 signedness/product cases and the 6+6*4+2 divider decomposition\n  on 65,444 RV32M quotient/remainder cases, including boundary values and\n  fixed-seed random inputs. This validates arithmetic decomposition only;\n  candidate RTL correctness, handshakes and Gowin timing are unmeasured.\n  Run all existing unit, unchanged formal and stalled cosimulation gates,\n  canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks, and vendor median Fmax. Keep\n  every existing divider latency and branch-followed-by-division progress\n  assertion, including the nine-cycle ready-bus retirement-gap bound and\n  unchanged formal liveness depth. Exercise mixed dependent M instructions\n  so the new multiplier cannot create an extra divider launch bubble.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mul_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the blocking two-service-edge multiplier with four registered\n      signed-limb partial products, balanced recombination, exact low/high\n      RV32M result selection and stable result/accept handshake. Preserve\n      exact ALTOPS formulas with identical latency. Capture original\n      operands for ALTOPS, reject busy restarts and cancel on reset.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a default-enabled static multiplication parameter/generate split.\n      The core disables both combinational M datapaths; standalone defaults\n      preserve existing arithmetic interfaces and tests. Ensure no 32x32\n      multiply remains synthesized behind the core's fast ALU result mux.\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep the existing states and eight-edge handshake, consuming six\n      narrow bits at launch, four bits on each of six recurrence edges,\n      and two final full-width bits before sign/exception correction in\n      DONE. Keep final result availability after edge seven, stable held\n      completion, exact 33-bit borrow semantics and ALTOPS schedule.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Instantiate the multiplier and disable fast-ALU multiplication.\n      Generalize execute_wait, start/accept, source snapshots and result\n      selection for legal MUL-family or DIV/REM instructions. Keep normal\n      fast EX behavior, older dmem priority, zero-control drain bubbles,\n      forwarding, branch recovery, metadata and once-only completion.\n  - file: test/test_mul_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Test real and ALTOPS multiplication through the sequential interface\n      for every MUL variant, all sign pairs, zero, INT_MIN, extrema,\n      16-bit limb/carry boundaries and randomized operands against an\n      independent integer reference. Verify result availability after\n      capture and acceptance on edge two, held-result stability, busy\n      launch rejection, back-to-back operations and reset cancellation.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve the complete existing default-mode arithmetic suite and\n      verify the added static parameter disables multiplication only in\n      the intended fast instance without altering non-M operations or\n      existing combinational-division parameter behavior.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve every existing real/ALTOPS result, latency and handshake\n      assertion. Add cases around prologue-width rejection boundaries,\n      signed magnitude extremes and final-two-bit quotient transitions;\n      check stable final corrected results across long held DONE periods.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add mul_unit.sv to explicit RTL source lists. Exercise all MUL\n      variants with forwarded operands and dependent ALU, branch, load,\n      store, multiply and divide consumers, x0 destinations, wrong-path\n      operations and independent memory stalls. Check source snapshots,\n      once-only side effects/retirement and reset during both M operations.\n      Preserve divider edge-eight acceptance, nine-cycle progress checks,\n      decoder/trap assertions and existing queue/predictor behavior.\n      Verify structurally that the core fast ALU elaborates no multiply,\n      division or remainder operator while the registered M units remain.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r5s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261003-002-r5s1' and d['category']=='structural'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int and -50 <= d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct'] <= 50\nprint(f'{p}: schema valid; {len(d[\\\"changes\\\"])} permitted change paths')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
