{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r8s1.yaml')\nx={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r8s1',\n'title':'Retime forwarding source selection and writeback data before EX',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 173.56 iterations/s at 70.074 MHz and 4,037,538 CoreMark bracket cycles. The last structural winner registered dependency comparisons but left producer write qualification and source priority after those registers. WB also still selects load versus ALU data after MEM/WB. Both control muxes precede EX operand selection and arithmetic. The reported champion path starts at an EX/MEM producer write control and reaches memory through the general ALU. A scratch attempt to separate memory addresses reached only 62.905 MHz; registering forwarding source enables alone reached 65.910 MHz and exposed the WB result mux as the next critical-path source. Retiming both parts of operand delivery is the single structural hypothesis.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Complete the forwarding network's existing register boundaries: compute final one-hot source enables before ID/EX, and select the architectural writeback value before MEM/WB. EX then receives registered RF/EX-MEM/MEM-WB source enables and a directly registered WB value. Keep the pipeline depth, all instruction service schedules and the load-use interlock unchanged. Do not add a predictor, cache, extra execute stage or change arithmetic algorithms.\n\nFor each incoming ID source, form an EX hit using the current ID/EX destination and EX's final write permission after its existing control-transfer alignment/trap checks. Form a WB hit using the current EX/MEM destination and MEM's final write permission after its existing memory alignment/trap checks. Qualify both by the corresponding producer's valid and nonzero destination. At the ordinary ID/EX capture edge register the mutually exclusive source vector {WB_hit && \"'!EX_hit, EX_hit, !(EX_hit || WB_hit)}. These are final priority-resolved enables, not address matches subsequently qualified by EX/MEM or MEM/WB controls. Preserve the existing one-hot operand data networks and all raw source values for stores, branches, M-unit capture and RVFI.\n\nExpose internal combinational producer-write eligibility signals from EX and MEM; do not add top-level ports. Use the controls that will actually enter the next producer register, so a trapped or illegal producer never overrides an older architectural register value. Registering decoded reg_write alone is insufficient. Ordinary ID capture happens only when those stages can advance. Mirror the current reset/flush/hold/invalid priorities exactly. Reset, permitted flush and invalid capture select RF. A dmem hold retains both complete vectors. During an M wait while older stages drain, translate an EX-selected source to WB and every other source to RF, matching the existing producer-stage movement. M-unit source snapshots already retain operands after launch. Never recompute a held instruction'\"'s enables from the changing fetch-queue head, and never gate retained WB data by retirement valid.\n\nAdd a unified writeback_value field to MEM/WB. In MEM compute it from load_data versus EX/MEM alu_result using the existing mem_to_reg control, and register the selected word on the existing MEM/WB edge. WB w_data becomes a direct wire from this field for register-file write-first bypass, EX forwarding and RVFI rd_wdata. Hold writeback_value with every other payload field when dmem stalls clear only retirement valid; reset initializes it. Preserve raw memory data and other RVFI metadata. The existing per-instruction write permission still controls actual writes, including misaligned loads. The legacy split result fields may remain only where needed; synthesis must not leave the old post-register result mux in the timed WB data path.\n\nPreserve nret=1, top-level IO, contiguous retirement, default-illegal decoding, memory side-effect gating, M-unit real and ALTOPS arithmetic, and every existing progress/latency check. Expected bracket cycles remain 4,037,538. Acceptance needs over 181.54376 iterations/s, approximately 73.30 MHz at this count. The forecast is ten percent, requiring approximately 77.08 MHz. Risks are moving exception qualification into the ID capture cone, longer memory-to-WB capture timing, and another arithmetic or branch path becoming limiting. Run unchanged formal, full RVFI cosimulation, real/ALTOPS pipeline suites and canonical stalled CoreMark validation, then compare median Gowin Fmax times the measured bracketed cycle rate. Combined-design timing and correctness validation are still pending.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':10,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'Move producer write qualification and youngest-writer priority before the ID/EX edge, registering final three-bit one-hot source vectors. Use EX/MEM-stage post-trap eligibility for the producers that become EX/MEM and MEM/WB respectively. Mirror reset, flush, invalid, dmem-hold and M-wait producer movement exactly; eliminate post-register reg_write qualification.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire internal final producer-write eligibility from EX and MEM into the registered forwarding unit. Preserve destination/source addresses, data routes, pipeline controls and all external ports.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Expose in.valid AND ctrl_with_trap.reg_write as the eligibility of the producer entering EX/MEM, after existing control-transfer alignment checks. Keep operand muxes, arithmetic, M-unit schedules, redirects and payload advancement unchanged.'},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Expose in.valid AND ctrl_with_trap.reg_write for the producer entering MEM/WB after memory alignment checks. Select and register writeback_value from load_data or alu_result before MEM/WB. Preserve payload retention when retirement valid clears under dmem holds and preserve all memory/RVFI behavior.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add a 32-bit unified writeback_value field to mem_wb_t; preserve required metadata and raw RVFI memory fields.'},\n {'file':'rtl/wb_stage.sv','description':'Drive w_data directly from registered writeback_value; retain valid-qualified reg_write and the architectural destination. Remove the timed post-MEM/WB load-versus-ALU data mux.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain all existing tests. Add competing same-destination producers, both-source and x0 cases, trapped producer write suppression, WB-to-ID bypass, and dependencies on every load width. Exercise held WB values during dmem stalls, M waits, reset and redirects. Check exact RVFI operands/results/order and once-only memory effects in real and ALTOPS modes, retaining all existing latency and progress bounds.'}\n]}\njsonschema.validate(x,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(x,sort_keys=False,width=86))\nprint('VALID',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
