{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_69","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r9s1.yaml'); h=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nh['title']='Precompute conditional-branch recovery before the ID/EX boundary'\ns=h['hypothesis']\ns=s.replace('and branch-training eligibility derived from the direct target alignment.', 'branch-training eligibility derived from direct target alignment, and the single correction destination implied by the captured prediction.')\ns=s.replace('Register only the small control metadata; retain current target and PC+4 datapaths in EX rather than adding wide target registers to this proposal.', '''Register one branch_recovery_target alongside this metadata: predicted_taken ? PC+4 : PC+branch_immediate. A direction correction for a conditional branch necessarily selects the opposite direction from its captured prediction. Consequently its correction destination is known in ID, before the actual comparison. Select this registered destination for a branch correction instead of using late actual_taken to select a 32-bit recovery target in EX. This is the essential data half of the same branch-recovery retiming. Do not register two separate target and PC+4 payloads. Keep the full existing architectural target/next-PC calculation for trap and RVFI purposes, and keep JALR's dynamic target computation.''')\ns=s.replace('The existing actual branch/jump target and correction target selection remain architectural and full-width.', 'Correction-target selection gives consumer replay highest priority, then the registered conditional-branch recovery destination, then the existing jump/fallthrough destination. All targets remain architectural and full-width.')\ns=s.replace('The final proposal additionally retimes outcome and training controls and separates the jump correction cone, targeting remaining serial branch 