{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r10s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Speculate narrow ordered-branch verification with selective correction'\nd['motivation']='''The champion's reported worst path includes forwarded EX operands, branch verification and fetch/decode recovery. Delaying every branch correction in r7s2 increased cycles without enough clock benefit, and r9s2's predecoded recovery predicates still regressed. An ISA profile of the canonical marker-bounded CoreMark run counts 151,852 ordered branches; 135,261 (89.1 percent) have identical upper 24 operand bits. Their exact signed or unsigned ordering is therefore determined by an 8-bit unsigned comparison. Predict this narrow ordering in the fast recovery path and register exact full-operand validation for selective correction.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Add one static operand-width predictor for BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU verification. Speculate that rs1[31:8] equals rs2[31:8], so unsigned rs1[7:0] < rs2[7:0] predicts less-than for both signed and unsigned ordered branches. Apply funct3 bit-zero inversion for BGE/BGEU. Use this speculative branch condition, legal instruction qualification and direct-target alignment to perform the existing same-cycle comparison against the saved fetch predicted_taken bit. Correct fetch immediately if the narrow condition disagrees with that bit. BEQ/BNE retain exact equality verification; JAL/JALR retain their existing behavior. Keep the fetch table, instruction-derived direct targets and queue unchanged.\n\nIn parallel compute the exact branch condition from full architectural forwarded operands. A split implementation may select low-byte ordering when the upper 24 bits are equal, otherwise signed/unsigned upper-24 ordering. Register a correction-pending bit with EX/MEM when the speculative effective transfer direction differs from the exact effective direction. Effective direction includes existing illegal/misaligned-target suppression: compare actual next-path choices, not raw relations. Capture the exact architectural outcome, source operands, trap and pc_next in the branch's EX/MEM entry. An unequal upper word alone is not a failure: if narrow and full comparisons give the same direction, no extra recovery occurs.\n\nOnly the narrow comparison feeds the same-cycle branch redirect. The full comparison and upper equality must not qualify execute_wait, fast redirect, fetch acceptance or ID flush. A guarded prototype that waited in EX whenever upper bits differed passed existing pipeline tests, all 53 formal checks and CoreMark CRCs, but measured only 61.549 MHz median because the validation guard lengthened stall feedback. This proposal specifically removes that guard from common-path advancement and registers recovery instead. Preserve exact once-only training from the validated outcome; never train using the narrow speculative result.\n\nOn the following cycle a registered correction-pending branch is the oldest instruction in MEM. Its registered actual pc_next overrides every younger EX correction or operand replay and redirects fetch even when imemReady is low. Flush ID/EX and empty both fetch-queue entries. Prevent the younger EX instruction from entering EX/MEM, launching or accepting an M operation, redirecting, training or making memory effects. Suppress a younger execute_wait so it cannot block this older correction. A wrong-path store has not reached MEM and must never issue a request. Reset cancels correction state.\n\nService this correction for exactly one edge while retaining the resolving branch's EX/MEM entry and clearing its pending bit. During this edge, make MEM/WB clear valid while retaining its old payload, using the existing forwarding-retention behavior. On the next edge the retained branch retires exactly once. This moves the branch retirement with its rare extra recovery cycle and preserves the established branch-to-divider retirement-gap bound. Do not retire the branch twice or leave a pending correction recurring. Normal branches, including correct width predictions, keep the existing stage schedule. A branch is not a memory operation, so its correction-service cycle cannot itself depend on dmemReady.\n\nCapture correction state only on valid EX advancement with validated operand-source permissions. Source-prediction replay must precede width prediction for an instruction whose operands are invalid. Existing older dmem holds retain the complete branch and prevent advancement, training and correction capture. Gate younger M launch and completion acceptance on the registered older correction before state changes. Leave load-use interlocks, ordinary forwarding, divider/multiplier schedules and all arithmetic outputs unchanged.\n\nUse actual full-operand outcomes for RVFI and traps, including taken misaligned branches. A speculative taken choice must be suppressed for a misaligned direct target even if the actual branch is untaken; both architectural and speculative next paths then fall through, with the actual trap bit determined only by the true condition. Preserve full 32-bit target addition, all fetch range checks, nret=1, exact default-illegal decode, retirement order, bounded memory accesses and all canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks.\n\nThe profile also found ideal sequential branch misses of 51,857 for the current bimodal table versus 38,052 for a much larger 1,024-entry gshare table. Those counts omit queue timing and are not RTL cycle measurements; they motivate targeting verification timing rather than table size. The proposed width speculation is program independent and must not contain benchmark PCs, operand constants or profiling-derived exception tables.\n\nForecast 7 percent higher fitness with low confidence, from shorter common branch verification. Selective correction adds cycles only when the speculative effective direction is wrong; arbitrary code may fail the width prediction much more frequently. Placement, validation/training routing and the remaining divider/ALU paths are material risks. Measure actual bracket cycles and all three vendor placement options. Acceptance requires more than 191.37616 iterations/s. Preserve all existing liveness and retirement-gap assertions; do not weaken checks to accommodate the new schedule.'''\nd['changes']=[{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Implement an 8-bit speculative ordered-branch condition for immediate direction verification and full-operand exact validation in parallel. Capture a correction-pending bit and exact architectural branch data at EX/MEM. Give registered older correction priority over all younger execution, replay, M launch/accept and training. Retain the branch during its single correction-service edge, then clear pending and allow once-only retirement. Export correction_service to MEM/WB hold wiring; never put the upper-equality guard in execute_wait. Preserve exact training, traps, forwarded RVFI operands, targets and all existing arithmetic schedules.'''},{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire EX's registered older-branch correction_service into MEM/WB's existing hold_wb behavior so correction service clears retirement validity while retaining prior forwarding payload. Keep the original dmem hold qualification, hazard interfaces, top-level IO and single retirement channel unchanged.'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Add all four ordered branch kinds with both fetch predictions and narrow-versus-exact agreement/disagreement, including equal and unequal upper 24 bits, low-byte borrow boundaries, opposite signs and INT_MIN. Test false early corrections followed by exact repair, correct early choices despite unequal upper bits, misaligned taken/untaken targets, all forwarding sources, source-permission replay, data/instruction stalls, full queues and reset. Place stores, jumps and MUL/DIV immediately behind a width-error branch; assert squash before side effects and M launch, exactly one training and retirement, exact RVFI operands/traps/next PC/order, and existing progress/retirement-gap bounds. Keep every existing correctness gate and compare canonical stalled CoreMark cycles plus median Gowin Fmax.'''}]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=85))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
