{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"agent_message","text":"None of the scratch variants beat the champion. I’m refining the final proposal to predict cached direct targets from the PC alone, then validate the cached target’s immediate and instruction kind in decode. A mismatch would discard younger fetches while letting the current instruction advance, preserving direction-only EX recovery. This version remains unmeasured.\n\n"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_46", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r11s2.yaml')\nx={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-003-r11s2',\n'title':'PC-only direct-target prediction with decode identity validation',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The current predictor feeds fetched instruction bits through a full-width target adder and eligibility checks into the fetch PC. Prior r2s2 cached targets but required general next-PC comparison in EX; r3s2 instead gained from exact direct targets and direction-only recovery. Recent changes to EX verification and recovery latency have regressed. Use a cached direct-target prediction independent of instruction data, with precise target-identity validation in decode, so EX retains its established direction-only check.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Add one 64-entry direct-mapped direct-target predictor indexed by fetch PC[7:2]. Each entry holds valid, source tag PC[19:8], target[19:2], a signed immediate identity key imm[20:1], and conditional-versus-JAL kind. Only aligned source PCs and full-width targets in [0,0x00100000) may be installed. Keep the current 64-entry two-bit direction table and its EX training schedule. On a tagged hit, predict the cached target for JAL or when the conditional direction counter predicts taken. On a miss predict PC+4. JALR is never installed. Source range/alignment, PC tag, entry validity, cached kind and the direction counter determine the IF prediction; raw imem_data must not qualify the fetch target selection. Remove the IF immediate-target adder entirely, including miss fallbacks.\n\nCarry the cached immediate identity key and cached kind with the exact fetched token through both queue entries and the empty-queue bypass, alongside predicted_taken. A token's metadata must use the same pre-edge cache contents as its PC decision, including simultaneous lookup and replacement. Unpredicted tokens need no valid identity key. Do not add a stage, disable the fall-through bypass, or hold fetch for validation. Use resettable valid bits with unreset payload arrays and a banked memory-friendly implementation. The logical cache state is 3328 bits (64 times 52), excluding the unchanged direction counters.\n\nIn ID validate every predicted-taken token against its actual instruction. Positively recognize a legal conditional branch or JAL, verify the cached kind, and compare the actual sign-extended B/J immediate[20:1] to the saved key. The immediate must be fully represented: a B immediate is sign-extended to the same 21-bit signed domain as JAL before dropping its implicit zero bit. Source PC identity was checked in IF. Equal source PC, immediate and direct-transfer kind guarantee the exact same target; changes to branch source registers or legal branch funct3 do not require a miss because they cannot change that target. Illegal branch funct3, JALR and non-control instructions fail validation. This avoids both a target adder and a full next-PC comparator in decode; it also keeps a general target comparison out of EX.\n\nWhen a predicted-taken token fails identity validation AND is actually accepted into ID/EX, redirect IF to that token's full 32-bit PC+4 and invalidate both younger queue entries. Accept the current instruction normally, but store predicted_taken=0 in its ID/EX entry. Do not replay the current instruction or reread its instruction memory: accepting it once prevents stale entries or symbolic changing instructions from causing an endless refetch loop. EX then resolves this instruction using the original direction-only machinery. A real taken branch, JAL or JALR can produce a later normal EX redirect; a non-control or illegal instruction follows its normal sequential/trap behavior. A successful validation retains the fetched predicted_taken bit exactly.\n\nThis decode correction is NOT an EX flush. Separate the two recovery sources: an older EX redirect or operand replay has priority, kills ID acceptance and retains its existing ID/EX flush behavior. A decode identity correction flushes IF occupancy and corrects the fetch PC while allowing that same accepted instruction and its forwarding selectors to enter EX. Qualify decode correction with actual decode acceptance, so load-use interlocks, older data-memory stalls and M-unit holds defer it. The combined redirect sent to IF must not feed back into hazard_unit's decode_accept or flush_id; otherwise it would suppress the very acceptance that authorizes the decode correction and form a loop. Compute decode correction after the existing EX-only acceptance decision. Reset wins over everything. IF correction still overrides an unready instruction bus.\n\nTrain the target predictor once from a valid legal direct transfer advancing from EX/MEM to MEM/WB. Use its existing registered PC, instruction, branch_target and final trap-qualified control. branch_target is the full PC+immediate sum for both branches and JAL. Decode the immediate identity key from this registered instruction with wiring only. Check source and target high bits and alignment before narrowing the stored target; never wrap an out-of-range target into memory. Install eligible untaken conditional branches as well as taken ones. Exclude JALR, bubbles, illegal/faulting transfers, reset and held entries. A branch already in MEM is older than a younger EX or ID redirect and must still train when advancing. Existing exact EX direction-counter training remains unchanged. No second cache write port or decode-side invalidation is needed for correctness: stale entries are always checked against the actual accepted instruction, and subsequent legal direct transfers refresh them.\n\nPreserve full architectural arithmetic and trap handling in EX, actual RVFI pc_next and operands, single-channel nret=1 IO, retirement order, load-use behavior, forwarding replay, M schedules, bounded addresses and all canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks. A cached speculative target is always aligned and bounded even when its instruction identity is stale. A wrong-path store must be removed before it reaches EX/MEM. The new ID correction must not suppress the older resolving instruction, train a squashed token, lose a queue entry, duplicate retirement or block forward progress under arbitrary symbolic code.\n\nThe expected benefit is shorter fetch prediction timing without the full EX next-PC comparison of r2s2. The main risks are the new decode-correction path through the empty-queue bypass, cache lookup routing, cold/conflict misses, and unchanged divider/ALU timing limits. Forecast 6 percent higher fitness with low confidence. The final arrangement is unmeasured; acceptance still requires more than 191.37616 iterations/s, approximately 77.27 MHz at the champion's 4037538 bracket cycles, with the clock requirement increasing proportionally if cycles rise.\n\nEvidence and limits: a scratch cache with full instruction equality gating prediction directly in IF validated canonical stalled CoreMark but measured 4044158 bracket cycles and only 69.247/68.725/68.725 MHz across Gowin placements. It is a regression and is not the proposed validation boundary. It shows modest miss overhead but no clock advantage. The final proposal moves a compact immediate/kind identity check to accepted decode and makes the speculative IF target independent of imem_data; that combination has not been built or verified. Do not claim measured improvement from the scratch result. Run unchanged formal and real/ALTOPS unit suites, stalled RVFI cosim, canonical CoreMark validation and all vendor placements on the final implementation; preserve existing liveness and retirement-gap assertions.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Implement the 64-entry PC-tagged direct-target cache and PC-only lookup, retaining the current bimodal direction table. Carry the saved immediate key/kind with each complete fetch token. Replace immediate-derived predicted targets with cached targets, predict fall-through on misses, and preserve pre-edge token association, queue behavior and correction priority. Accept eligible registered installations from MEM. Only valid bits reset; invalid payloads must never affect a prediction.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Extend if_id_t with the cached signed-immediate identity key and conditional/JAL kind. ID consumes this metadata before EX; do not add a general predicted-next-PC comparison field to id_ex_t. Preserve existing architectural metadata and top-level RVFI shape.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Validate a predicted-taken token using positive legal direct-transfer decode, cached kind and exact sign-extended immediate key. Export an identity-mismatch indication. On actual acceptance capture predicted_taken AND identity_valid; preserve existing reset, older EX flush and hold priorities. A mismatch must not discard or reread the current instruction.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Generate an ID correction only from actual EX-qualified decode_accept and ID's identity mismatch. Send IF the older EX correction preferentially, otherwise ID's PC+4 correction; keep hazard/ID flush driven by EX correction only, avoiding acceptance feedback. Keep forwarding selector capture for the accepted ID correction instruction. Wire target-cache installations from registered eligible advancing EX/MEM direct transfers, checking full-width bounds/alignment and decoding the exact immediate key. Preserve top-level ports and original direction training.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'''Update packed-token extraction and the predictor reference for PC-only cache hits/misses. Preserve all queue, counter and range assertions. Check reset, cold misses, tag collisions, bounded cache installations, simultaneous training/lookup and immutable cached identity metadata across queue holds. Explicitly show IF prediction can use a stale identity while its target remains bounded; pipeline tests must prove mandatory decode repair.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Model the one-stage-later target installation and accepted decode identity correction. Cover stale opcode/kind/immediate, illegal branch funct3, unchanged target with changed source registers or legal branch funct3, same-PC instruction changes, JALR exclusion, cold/conflict misses, misaligned and out-of-range resolved targets, raw bypass and both queue entries. Test ID repair simultaneous with an older EX redirect/replay, load-use/data/M holds, unready imem, reset and full queues. Assert current-instruction acceptance exactly once, younger squash before stores or M launch, precise RVFI PC/trap/order, one installation per eligible MEM advancement and no combinational acceptance loop. Retain every existing liveness/retirement-gap check, including stale predictions immediately before DIV/REM, and update only predictor-policy and packed-field expectations.'''},\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(x,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(x,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert -50<=x['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']<=50\nprint('Wrote and schema-validated',p)\nprint('Files:',', '.join(z['file'] for z in x['changes']))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
