{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_68","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python -B - <<'PY'\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/561ff4c6/34347d76/snapshot32');r=build(Path.cwd(),p/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',1,p/'gowin1');(p/'timing1.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2));print(json.dumps(r))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\"place_option\": 1, \"placement_failed\": false, \"fmax_mhz\": 70.947, \"levels\": 13, \"logic\": 5114, \"regs\": 1871, \"lutram\": 0, \"bsram\": 4, \"dsp\": 2, \"critical_paths\": [{\"from\": \"cpu/u_fwd/fwd_rs1_2_s1\", \"to\": \"dmem_3_dmem_3_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -9.095, \"arrival_ns\": 19.39, \"logic_cells\": 13, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, {\"from\": \"cpu/u_fwd/fwd_rs1_3_s1\", \"to\": \"dmem_3_dmem_3_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -9.078, \"arrival_ns\": 19.373, \"logic_cells\": 13, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, {\"from\": \"cpu/u_id/reg_q.ctrl.is_auipc_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_3_dmem_3_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -9.064, \"arrival_ns\": 19.359, \"logic_cells\": 11, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, {\"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_div/quotient_30_s1\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_1_s1\", \"slack_ns\": -9.064, \"arrival_ns\": 19.358, \"logic_cells\": 71, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_div\"]}, {\"from\": \"cpu/u_fwd/fwd_rs2_1_s1\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_if/tail_q.instr_25_s0\", \"slack_ns\": -9.06, \"arrival_ns\": 19.354, \"logic_cells\": 35, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\"]}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r13s2.yaml')\nd=dict(id='hyp-20261003-003-r13s2',title='Train a compact bimodal predictor from fetched counter snapshots',category='predictor')\nd['motivation']='''The current direction table reads counters at both the fetch PC and the resolving branch index: training performs an asynchronous read-modify-write of the current entry. This adds selection and feedback logic and inhibits a simple one-read/one-write memory implementation. Prior recovery restructuring regressed and the PC-only target cache timed out in formal, so preserve instruction-derived targets and same-cycle EX correction. A scratch 32-entry folded-PC two-bit table reduced canonical CoreMark cycles to 4,034,312 but reached only 72.658 MHz in placement option 1. A one-bit version removed the training read, inferred six RAM16 resources and reached 75.191 MHz in that option, but loses hysteresis. The proposed single predictor mechanism removes the training read while retaining two-bit direction state.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Replace the 64-entry bimodal table with a 32-entry, two-bit snapshot-trained direction predictor. Index fetch and training with PC[6:2] XOR PC[11:7]. At fetch capture the complete raw two-bit counter alongside the existing predicted_taken bit in the exact bus token, both queue entries and the empty-queue bypass. Propagate this snapshot through IF/ID and ID/EX. It is separate from predicted_taken: target eligibility and instruction class can suppress predicted_taken even when the raw counter MSB is one.\n\nAt the existing valid, legal, nonfaulting EX branch advancement, saturate the saved counter toward the exact branch outcome and unconditionally write that resulting two-bit value to the resolving branch's hashed index. For taken use min(snapshot+1,3); for not-taken use max(snapshot-1,0). Write saturation endpoints too: suppressing an endpoint write based on the saved value would leave a potentially different aliased current entry unchanged. Do not read counters[training_index], compare current and saved states, forward a newer update, or add a second read port. The deliberate microarchitectural prediction is that the fetched counter remains a useful estimate of its current state. Intervening branches can make the snapshot stale; accepting this approximate learning is safe because predictor state never authorizes architectural effects. Every actual instruction still resolves against its own saved predicted_taken with exact forwarded operands and full architectural target arithmetic.\n\nUse one asynchronous lookup port and one synchronous training write port. Keep reset to weakly not taken (01), with reset priority over writes, using synthesis-friendly storage. Check Gowin mapping rather than assuming a small array becomes RAM. A logical reset implementation may use validity and default 01 if needed, but every invalid entry must read a defined 01 immediately after reset and every first write must install the complete state. Do not scrub entries over extra cycles or weaken reset checks. No new state is tied to benchmark PCs, operands, marker activity, or stall-generator phase.\n\nBoth next-PC selection and the counter snapshot captured with a fetched token use the same pre-edge lookup. A simultaneous same-index training write affects later fetches only; a stored queue token never refreshes its snapshot. If the queue is full or imem is unready, no new fetch token is accepted. A held ID/EX branch retains all metadata. Reset and corrections discard snapshots with their tokens. Train exactly once on the existing branch_train event; no training on invalid, illegal, misaligned, replayed, held, squashed or incomplete-M instructions. No rollback is needed for table writes from advancing older branches. Aliasing and stale learning may change prediction accuracy but must never change instruction identity or actual retirement.\n\nKeep JAL prediction unconditional when its full instruction-derived target is eligible, keep JALR unpredicted, and preserve the complete full-width source/target range and alignment checks. Keep the two-entry queue, recovery edge, forwarding prediction/replay, load-use interlocks, memory handshake and M schedules unchanged. Do not introduce a cached target or a general predicted-next-PC equality check. Preserve nret=1, public IO, default-illegal decode, traps, RVFI operands/next-PC/order, bounded memory accesses, progress and all canonical CRC/UART/timing-marker gates.\n\nThe intended benefit is eliminating the indexed training-state read and enabling a compact one-read/one-write direction memory while retaining hysteresis. A sequential reference trace gave 50,576 conditional misses for the 32-entry folded two-bit policy versus 51,857 for the current table; this model does not include stale snapshots, queue timing or bus stalls and is not a prediction of final RTL cycles. Stale updates, extra token metadata, memory inference and unchanged divider/ALU/recovery paths are the main risks. Forecast six percent higher fitness with low confidence. The scratch measurements above are below the acceptance threshold and do not establish a win. Measure the final snapshot policy's actual bracket cycles and all three Gowin placements. Acceptance requires fitness above 191.37616 iterations/s; the required Fmax is 191.37616 times measured cycles divided by 10,000,000 in MHz.\n\nAdd independent cycle-accurate tests of the explicitly changed learning rule, including deliberate alias overwrite between fetch and EX, two in-flight instances of the same branch, saturation endpoints and same-edge lookup/write. Update only predictor-policy expectations and necessary packed-field offsets in existing tests; retain every architectural, progress, latency, squash and memory assertion. Run unchanged formal, real and ALTOPS pipeline tests, stalled RVFI cosimulation and the canonical CoreMark validator. No repository RTL is delivered during hypothesis generation; the hypothesis is the implementation request.'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes']=[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Implement the 32-entry folded-PC direction memory with one fetch lookup and snapshot-derived training writes. Capture raw two-bit state in complete queue tokens and bypass output, preserve pre-edge semantics, and keep all direct-target, queue and correction logic unchanged. Reset every logical state to 01.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add a two-bit predictor_state field to if_id_t and id_ex_t, separate from predicted_taken. Preserve architectural and downstream RVFI fields.'},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Capture and hold the selected token counter snapshot with the complete ID/EX bundle using existing reset/flush/hold/bubble priorities.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Export the saved predictor_state with the existing exact once-per-advance branch training event; compute the matching folded PC index. Keep actual branch outcomes, correction timing, traps, forwarding replay and M schedules unchanged.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire the two-bit saved-state feedback from EX to IF without adding a training or recovery pipeline stage. Preserve all public ports and nret=1 retirement behavior.'},\n {'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'Model snapshot-trained saturating updates independently. Cover all eight state/outcome transitions, endpoints, reset, every folded index, collisions, pre-edge same-index reads/writes, queue full/empty behavior, held tokens and correction invalidation. Verify exact PC/instruction/prediction/snapshot coherence.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Exercise branches whose table entries change between fetch and EX, short repeated-PC loops, colliding PCs, load-dependent branches, both recovery directions, misaligned and illegal encodings, operand replay and simultaneous imem/dmem stalls. Adapt predictor scoreboards and packed offsets while retaining exact retirement, RVFI, trap, memory, squash, M-latency and progress checks.'}\n]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=86))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('VALID',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
