{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261003-001-r11s0','hyp-20261003-001-r12s0']:\n  print(d['id'],d.get('fmax_mhz'),d.get('cycles'),d.get('outcome'),str(d.get('error'))[:900]);print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:2300])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261003-001-r11s0 72.899 4037538 regression None\nFactor ID/EX squashing into the instruction-valid bit alone, removing\nflush and fetch validity from decode payload and registered forwarding\nsource-mask updates. This is one local control-fanout optimization.\nPreserve all stage boundaries, instruction latencies, fetch behavior,\nbranch recovery timing, source priority and M-unit schedules.\n\nIn id_stage split valid from the remaining payload assignments. For\nvalid preserve exactly reset/flush priority, then stall hold, otherwise\ncapture in.valid. For every other field reset to zero, hold on stall,\notherwise capture the raw decoded instruction, register values and\nmetadata regardless of flush or in.valid. Do not clear payload on a\nbubble and do not gate its enable with decode_accept: that would restore\nthe redirect dependency. A load-use flush with stall retains the old\npayload but invalidates it; an unstalled redirect may capture wrong-path\npayload with valid zero. Neither is an executing instruction.\n\nIn forward_unit reset source masks as before, but remove flush_id and\nid_valid from their sequential update conditions. On stall_id retain\nthe existing behavior: dmem hold retains masks; an M/load-use hold\nwithout stall_ex_mem performs the existing EX-to-WB source aging.\nOtherwise capture the existing four-way one-hot dependency prediction\nfrom the selected raw decode token. Masks accompanying an invalid\nID/EX entry may be nonzero and must be ignored. Never qualify the masks\nwith current valid or flush on their way to EX. Retain exact youngest\nproducer priority, separate WB ALU/load enables, retained WB forwarding\npermission and the existing canceled-write replay mechanism. Remove\nobsolete flush_id/id_valid ports and their core connections if unused.\n\nAt the core hazard connection qualify id_ex_mem_read with id_ex_w.valid.\nThis is mandatory because an invalid ID/EX payload may now retain load\ncontrol and a nonzero rd. The ID/EX write-intent input to forwarding\nalready includes valid and must retain that qualificat\n…[1638 of 4710 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4b345e9aa738e695]…\nof the complete EX/MEM register. On every other edge capture ALL non-control payload fields unconditionally using their existing expressions: PC, ALU/M/link result, raw store operand, register addresses, forwarded RVFI operands, resolved PC/branch metadata and instruction. Independently, clear ctrl and valid when operands_invalid || execute_wait || !in.valid; otherwise capture ctrl_with_trap and in.valid. Do not retain inactive payload on a bubble: that would put execute_wait, replay or validity back on its write enable. Valid and side-effect controls must still become zero on every bubble, replay and incomplete M operation. Early captures of an incomplete M result are harmless only with those controls and valid cleared; the actual completion edge still captures the correct held result and operand snapshots.\n\nApply the same separation to IF queue payloads. The queue already invalidates tokens through occupancy, but its payload assignments are still inside the reset/redirect branch and depend on redirect-qual\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
