{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_43", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r14s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261003-003-r14s2\ntitle: \\\"Predict branch bias disagreement with a compact one-bit table\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current 64-entry two-bit table stores 128 state bits and performs an\n  indexed read-modify-write at branch resolution. Recent snapshot-trained\n  RAM mapping saved only 0.08% of CoreMark cycles and regressed Fmax to\n  67.967 MHz. A smaller one-bit predictor removes the training read and\n  saturation logic without snapshot metadata or validity arrays. Encoding\n  disagreement with the fetched branch's backward/forward bias lets\n  branches with opposite common directions share a useful table state.\n  The intended benefit is a smaller, simpler predictor circuit rather\n  than an assumed increase in direction accuracy.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the existing bimodal predictor with one 32-entry one-bit agree\n  predictor. Its state bit means disagreement with backward-taken,\n  forward-not-taken bias. Index with PC[6:2] XOR PC[11:7]. Reset all 32\n  disagreement bits to zero in one edge. For a positively decoded legal\n  conditional branch, raw_direction = instruction[31] XOR disagreement[index].\n  For B instructions, instruction[31] is the sign of the displacement.\n  Keep JAL's existing unconditional taken prediction and JALR's unpredicted\n  behavior. Apply all existing source and full-width target range and\n  alignment checks after the raw direction decision; a rejected target\n  predicts fallthrough. Continue deriving targets from the actual fetched\n  instruction, never a cached target, truncated sum or aliased table entry.\n\n  On the existing valid, legal, nonfaulting advancing EX conditional-branch\n  training event, write actual_branch_outcome XOR branch_immediate[31] to\n  the matching folded-PC index. This is an unconditional one-bit overwrite,\n  with no training-index state read, increment/decrement, saturation or\n  confidence counter. Use the resolving branch's own sign bit and PC,\n  never the live fetch instruction. Add one scalar EX-to-IF training-bias\n  connection, while retaining branch_outcome as the exact outcome for\n  observability. Existing branch_index wiring may retain six bits with\n  bit 5 tied to zero, or consistently use five bits internally. No new\n  predictor metadata is needed in IF/ID or ID/EX: the existing captured\n  predicted_taken bit already records the actual fetch choice.\n\n  Initially use exactly 32 resettable state bits with one synchronous\n  write and one asynchronous lookup. Do not add RAM validity bits, stale\n  counter snapshots, tags, an initialization sweep, or vendor primitives.\n  A same-edge fetch and update use the old table value for both next-PC\n  selection and captured predicted_taken; subsequent fetches observe the\n  new value. A stored queue token must never refresh its prediction after\n  table updates. Preserve reset precedence, once-only training, exact\n  queue/bypass token coherence and existing hold/squash behavior. Updates\n  to aliased entries may hurt accuracy but cannot affect architectural\n  correctness, because EX still checks each saved prediction using exact\n  forwarded operands and its own full-width target.\n\n  Keep the entire existing same-cycle recovery boundary, two-entry fetch\n  queue, load-use interlocks, forwarding-source prediction/replay, memory\n  handshakes, multiplier/divider schedules and decoder untouched. Preserve\n  nret=1 and every public port, exact RVFI operands/traps/next-PC/order,\n  memory bounds, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker validation, and all\n  existing formal and forward-progress requirements. This proposal changes\n  only branch direction learning and its compact state representation.\n\n  A sequential reference trace measured 51,857 conditional misses for the\n  current 64-entry bimodal policy and 70,745 for this 32-entry agree policy;\n  an ordinary folded-PC 32-entry one-bit policy had 71,035. These counts\n  exclude pipeline timing, overlapping instances, bus stalls and speculative\n  fetches. They establish an accuracy cost, not a measured cycle count.\n  The hypothesis is that deleting the second state bit, indexed training\n  read and saturation network reduces clock period enough to outweigh\n  that cost. It must not be presented as an accuracy improvement over\n  the champion. Forecast 6% higher fitness with low confidence. At a\n  0.6% cycle increase, clearing the 4.6% acceptance margin requires about\n  77.73 MHz. The unchanged branch recovery, ALU and divider paths, loss\n  of hysteresis and physical placement may prevent a win.\n\n  Preserve all architectural test assertions. Adapt only expectations\n  that explicitly encode the old predictor policy, including reset\n  prediction, index mapping, counter transitions and warm-loop timing.\n  Keep the existing nine-cycle branch-to-divider progress bound and\n  unchanged formal liveness window. Test opposite-bias aliasing: a taken\n  backward branch and an untaken forward branch sharing an index both\n  train disagreement zero. Also test two branches with conflicting\n  disagreement, all legal predicates, both correction directions, target\n  eligibility overrides, same-edge training/lookup, repeated-PC tokens,\n  queue-full holds, reset after training, reserved encodings, misaligned\n  targets, canceled-write replay and independent bus stalls. Require exact\n  retirement order, operands, traps and once-only memory effects.\n\n  Run the unchanged formal suite, real/ALTOPS unit and pipeline tests,\n  reference-ISS cosimulation, canonical stalled CoreMark validation and\n  prescribed three-placement Gowin median. Acceptance requires more than\n  191.37616 iterations/s. No implementation or timing claim is implied by\n  this forecast; report final measured cycles and median clock explicitly.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace 64 two-bit counters with 32 resettable disagreement bits,\n      folded-PC indexing, bias-relative conditional prediction and direct\n      outcome/bias overwrite training. Preserve the queue, raw decode,\n      immediate-derived targets, eligibility checks and correction timing.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export the resolving branch's immediate sign as training bias and\n      compute the same folded-PC training index. Preserve exact outcome,\n      once-only training qualification, recovery, traps, replay and M units.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the scalar branch training bias between EX and IF and keep\n      index widths consistent. Preserve all external ports and RVFI.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Independently model the agree policy and check reset, all indices,\n      opposite-bias and conflicting aliases, same-edge writes/lookups,\n      target eligibility, queue/bypass coherence, holds and corrections.\n      Preserve architectural fetch and bounded-target assertions.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update policy-specific predictor scoreboards for folded indexing and\n      bias-relative one-bit writes. Cover alternating outcomes, both miss\n      directions, loaded operands, trap/replay and independent bus stalls.\n      Preserve exact RVFI/memory/progress checks and the nine-cycle bound.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r14s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()));print('Schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
