{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport sys,json\nsys.path.insert(0,'test/cosim')\nfrom reference import RV32IM\nfrom collections import Counter\nr=RV32IM();r.load_elf('bench/programs/coremark.elf');active=False;stats=Counter();bim=[1]*64\nvariants={n:([False]*64,[False]*n) for n in [4,8,16,32,64]}\nwhile r.retired<6000000:\n e=r.step()\n if e is None: break\n if e['mem_wmask'] and e['mem_addr']==0x10000100: active=True\n if e['mem_wmask'] and e['mem_addr']==0x10000104: break\n ins=e['insn'];pc=e['pc']\n if ins&127\"'!=0x63: continue\n out=e['\"'npc']\"'!=pc+4;idx=(pc>>2)&63\n if active: stats['\"'branches']+=1;stats['bimodal_miss']+=(bim[idx]>=2)\"'!=out\n bim[idx]=min(3,bim[idx]+1) if out else max(0,bim[idx]-1)\n for n,(p,h) in variants.items():\n  hi=idx&(n-1);old=p[idx]\n  if active: stats[f'\"'shared_{n}_miss']+=old\"'!=out\n  if not h[hi]: p[idx]=out\n  h[hi]=old==out\nprint(json.dumps(stats,indent=2));open('\"'/srv/hwebench/tmp/561ff4c6/17afaf14/shared_profile.json','w').write(json.dumps(stats))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport sys,json\nsys.path.insert(0,'test/cosim')\nfrom reference import RV32IM\nfrom collections import Counter\nr=RV32IM();r.load_elf('bench/programs/coremark.elf');active=False;stats=Counter();bim=[1]*64\nvariants={n:([False]*64,[False]*n) for n in [4,8,16,32,64]}\nwhile r.retired<6000000:\n e=r.step()\n if e is None: break\n if e['mem_wmask'] and e['mem_addr']==0x10000100: active=True\n if e['mem_wmask'] and e['mem_addr']==0x10000104: break\n ins=e['insn'];pc=e['pc']\n if ins&127\"'!=0x63: continue\n out=e['\"'npc']\"'!=pc+4;idx=(pc>>2)&63\n if active: stats['\"'branches']+=1;stats['bimodal_miss']+=(bim[idx]>=2)\"'!=out\n bim[idx]=min(3,bim[idx]+1) if out else max(0,bim[idx]-1)\n for n,(p,h) in variants.items():\n  hi=idx&(n-1);old=p[idx]\n  if active: stats[f'\"'shared_{n}_miss']+=old\"'!=out\n  if not h[hi]: p[idx]=out\n  h[hi]=old==out\nprint(json.dumps(stats,indent=2));open('\"'/srv/hwebench/tmp/561ff4c6/17afaf14/shared_profile.json','w').write(json.dumps(stats))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"branches\": 529588,\n  \"bimodal_miss\": 51857,\n  \"shared_4_miss\": 59955,\n  \"shared_8_miss\": 54913,\n  \"shared_16_miss\": 52119,\n  \"shared_32_miss\": 51835,\n  \"shared_64_miss\": 51857\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_50", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r15s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261003-003-r15s2\ntitle: \\\"Share hysteresis across a banked 64-entry direction predictor\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 64-entry bimodal predictor avoids many branch corrections,\n  but stores two state bits per direction entry and uses indexed saturating\n  updates. Recent 32-entry snapshot and one-bit agree predictors regressed\n  Fmax; the agree variant also increased sequential-reference misses from\n  51,857 to 70,745. Retain all 64 direction entries and retain hysteresis,\n  while sharing only the strength bits. This targets predictor area and\n  training timing without repeating a loss of direction capacity or adding\n  speculative operand comparisons to the execute stage.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the 64 ordinary two-bit counters with a shared-hysteresis bimodal\n  predictor: 64 prediction bits P and 16 strength bits H. Index P with\n  PC[7:2], exactly as today. H[j] is shared by P[j], P[j+16], P[j+32] and\n  P[j+48], where j is the low four index bits. H=1 means strong and H=0\n  means weak. Reset every P and H bit to zero, giving weakly not-taken\n  predictions immediately, without an initialization sweep or valid array.\n  Predict legal conditional branches from P[index]. Keep direct JAL taken\n  and JALR unpredicted, and retain all existing full-width source/target\n  bounds and alignment gates after the raw direction choice.\n\n  On a training update with actual outcome O, read the CURRENT prediction\n  bit old_P and its CURRENT shared strength old_H. Update the addressed\n  prediction bit to old_H ? old_P : O. Update the addressed shared strength\n  to (old_P == O), using the pre-update prediction. All other prediction\n  and strength bits hold. Without sharing, this is exactly the ordinary\n  two-bit saturating state machine recoded as direction and strength.\n  Sharing introduces interference only in resistance to changing direction;\n  it never writes another entry's P bit. Do not implement this as a one-bit\n  last-outcome predictor, arithmetic counter, or stale fetch-state update.\n\n  Decouple learning from EX with ONE pending training register containing\n  valid, six-bit index and actual outcome. Capture the existing branch_train,\n  branch_index and branch_outcome event each edge. On the next edge apply\n  the pending event to the current P/H state while independently accepting\n  the next event. Set pending valid to zero when no new event arrives.\n  Back-to-back updates, including the same P or H group, must apply in order\n  to current state; no event may be overwritten, merged or repeated. Reset\n  clears pending valid and predictor state. A captured event represents an\n  already advancing, legal, nonfaulting branch: its own redirect, a younger\n  redirect or subsequent bus/execute hold must not cancel its later update.\n  Only learning is delayed; verification and recovery remain same-cycle EX.\n\n  Organize storage as 16 constant-index groups, each with four separately\n  enabled P flops and one H flop. In each group, the two high training-index\n  bits select its old P through a local four-to-one mux for H's equality\n  update. The low four bits and pending valid enable only the selected\n  group's H. Each P flop has its own full-index match enable and uses its\n  group's directly wired H in its two-input update mux. This avoids a global\n  64-entry training-state read, saturation arithmetic, and a 64-bit pending\n  one-hot mask. Use ordinary synthesizable resettable flops, not vendor\n  primitives. Total logical state is 80 predictor bits plus 8 pending-event\n  bits, versus the champion's 128 predictor bits. Lookup first selects one\n  of four P bits per group with fetch PC[7:6], then selects the group with\n  fetch PC[5:2], using a balanced tree. Preserve this local update structure\n  rather than recreating an indexed two-bit counter array.\n\n  Fetch and a simultaneous table write use pre-edge P state consistently\n  for both the next-PC choice and captured predicted_taken. Preserve that\n  exact prediction in each queue token and ID/EX, including the empty-queue\n  bypass and arbitrarily long holds. Never refresh a stored token from the\n  live table. No new IF/ID or ID/EX metadata is needed. EX's training outputs\n  retain their current-cycle meanings; the pending register belongs in IF.\n\n  Keep exact forwarded branch resolution, direction-only correction, full\n  instruction-derived targets, immediate recovery priority, canceled-write\n  operand replay, load-use interlocks, all bus handshakes, and M-unit service\n  schedules unchanged. Keep default-illegal decode, misalignment traps,\n  link-write suppression, nret=1 IO, actual RVFI operands/PC/order and all\n  memory side-effect and forward-progress guarantees. Prediction state may\n  change performance only. A wrong prediction must never authorize a\n  wrong-path store, arithmetic launch or retirement.\n\n  The forecast is 6 percent higher fitness with LOW confidence, driven by\n  fewer state bits and a shorter, local learner rather than assumed higher\n  direction accuracy. H sharing and one-cycle-later training can increase\n  corrections, and unrelated ALU/divider/recovery paths may limit Fmax.\n  This final circuit has not been measured in RTL or Gowin; no demonstrated\n  fitness improvement is claimed. At unchanged 4,037,538 bracket cycles,\n  acceptance requires about 77.27 MHz median Fmax; any cycle increase raises\n  that requirement proportionally. Require measured fitness strictly above\n  191.37616 iterations/s with the prescribed median vendor evaluation.\n\n  Adapt only policy/state-specific test expectations. Preserve every\n  architectural assertion, the existing nine-cycle branch-to-divider gap,\n  formal liveness window and all canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks.\n  Run unchanged formal, real/ALTOPS pipeline tests, reference-ISS cosimulation\n  and stalled CoreMark validation before median Gowin evaluation. Include\n  all state/outcome transitions, strength sharing interference, both miss\n  directions, same-edge lookup/write, ordered pending events, reset during\n  a pending update, queue holds, invalid encodings, bounded speculative\n  targets, source replay and independent bus stalls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the 64 two-bit counters with 16 local groups containing four\n      direction bits and one shared strength bit. Add one valid/index/outcome\n      pending training register and implement current-state direction/strength\n      transitions with local four-way strength-update selection and individual\n      P enables. Use balanced prediction lookup. Preserve all existing module\n      ports, target eligibility, queue tokens, fetch acceptance and recovery.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Independently model 64 P bits, 16 H bits and the one-event training\n      pipeline. Check every transition and group, four PCs sharing H,\n      unaffected neighboring P bits, cold/reset behavior, back-to-back and\n      same-index updates, old-state lookup/write semantics and reset with a\n      pending event. Preserve queue, target-range, hold and correction checks.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update only predictor-state and prediction-policy scoreboards for\n      shared hysteresis and delayed IF writes. Keep current-cycle EX training\n      event assertions, precise RVFI/memory checks and existing progress\n      bounds. Check that an accepted event survives its own correction and\n      later holds exactly once, while squashed or held EX tokens cannot\n      generate extra events. Exercise conflicting shared-strength branches,\n      operand replay, both miss directions and simultaneous imem/dmem stalls.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r15s2.yaml';d=yaml.safe_load(open(p));jsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261003-003-r15s2' and d['category']=='predictor'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int and -50<=d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']<=50\nprint('Final candidate written and schema validated.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
