{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\nID', d['id'], 'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),'Fmax',d.get('fmax_mhz'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES',d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 fitness 12.75 cycles 4201766 Fmax 5.359\nAdd one compact fetch-stage next-PC predictor: a 64-entry, two-bit\nsaturating bimodal direction table indexed by PC[7:2], combined with\ntargets decoded directly from the fetched B/J immediate. Initialize all\ncounters to weakly not-taken (01); their MSB predicts conditional-branch\ndirection. Recognize only legal BRANCH funct3 values and JAL. Predict JAL\ntaken unconditionally when its target is eligible; JALR and all other\ninstructions predict PC+4. Use full 32-bit PC-relative arithmetic and\nrestrict speculative taken targets to word-aligned addresses in main\nmemory [0x00000000, 0x00100000). An ineligible target predicts fall-through\nand is resolved normally in EX. Keep the table inside if_stage.sv, with\n128 counter bits and no tags, target array, global history, or return stack.\n\nForm predictions from the raw imem_data and fetch PC, independently of\nthe flush-masked IF/ID instruction, to avoid combinational feedback.\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with the instruction through IF/ID\nand ID/EX. Only apply a prediction when the instruction is accepted:\nimem is ready, no load-use/data-memory hold exists, and no older EX\ncorrection squashes it. Prediction itself must not flush its instruction\nor its correctly predicted successor. Hold the captured prediction with\nthe rest of ID/EX during data-memory stalls.\n\nIn EX, compute resolved_next_pc using the existing forwarded comparison,\nJAL/JALR targets, and misalignment/trap behavior. Change redirect to\nin.valid && !stall && (resolved_next_pc != in.predicted_next_pc), and use\nresolved_next_pc as the correction target. This recovers both predicted\nnot-taken/actually-taken and predicted taken/actually-not-taken cases.\nCompare against the captured prediction, never a fresh table lookup or\nthe current fetch PC. RVFI pc_wdata remains the actual resolved next PC;\nJAL/JALR still write PC+4, and misaligned transfers retain the existing\ntrap and fall-through behavior.\n\nTrain the counter at the resolving instruction'\n…[10097 of 13169 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f68cf594a325440d]…\nbubbles, squashed instructions, held EX cycles or incomplete\ndivisions. Reset overrides training. A dmem-held branch defers both\ntraining and correction until it advances; prediction has no authority\nto commit side effects or retire instructions.\n\nThe forecast is an unmeasured 10% fitness gain, based on recovering the\nearlier direct predictor's approximately 6.45% cycle reduction and\navoiding the newly exposed correction-control timing bottleneck. Fetch\nimmediate addition, counter selection and placement remain timing\nrisks; direction-only recovery does not guarantee higher Fmax. At\n4,201,766 cycles, beating the 102.93 iter/s champion by more than 4.6%\nrequires median Fmax above approximately 45.24 MHz (fitness >107.665).\nValidate the existing unit, formal and stalled CoreMark cosimulation\ngates, all canonical CRCs and marker timing, then measure vendor median\nFmax and the actual product. Preserve nret=1, core ports, the divider,\ndecoder legality, architectural address rules and every eval invariant.\n\nNOTES \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
