{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'],d['title'], {k:d.get(k) for k in ['outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','error']})\n  print('MOTIVATION',d.get('motivation'))\n  if d['id'].endswith(('r1s2','r2s2')):\n   print('HYPOTHESIS',d['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis'))\n  print('NOTES',d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 Fetch-stage bimodal prediction with immediate-derived targets {'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 12.75, 'fmax_mhz': 5.359, 'cycles': 4201766, 'error': None}\nMOTIVATION The baseline predicts sequential execution and flushes fetch/decode for\nevery taken conditional branch and every JAL/JALR. Despite the five-stage\ndescription, IF/ID is combinational: each redirect discards one younger\nfetch slot. CoreMark's repeated loops and biased conditional branches\nshould provide enough predictable transfers to reduce measured cycles.\nThere are no predictor attempts in experiments/log.jsonl and no LESSONS.md.\nThe recorded 199.837 ns critical path runs through forwarding and the ALU,\nso this proposal targets iterations/cycle, with approximately unchanged\nFmax. The added predictor must remain small to limit placement disruption.\n\nHYPOTHESIS Add one compact fetch-stage next-PC predictor: a 64-entry, two-bit\nsaturating bimodal direction table indexed by PC[7:2], combined with\ntargets decoded directly from the fetched B/J immediate. Initialize all\ncounters to weakly not-taken (01); their MSB predicts conditional-branch\ndirection. Recognize only legal BRANCH funct3 values and JAL. Predict JAL\ntaken unconditionally when its target is eligible; JALR and all other\ninstructions predict PC+4. Use full 32-bit PC-relative arithmetic and\nrestrict speculative taken targets to word-aligned addresses in main\nmemory [0x00000000, 0x00100000). An ineligible target predicts fall-through\nand is resolved normally in EX. Keep the table inside if_stage.sv, with\n128 counter bits and no tags, target array, global history, or return stack.\n\nForm predictions from the raw imem_data and fetch PC, independently of\nthe flush-masked IF/ID instruction, to avoid combinational feedback.\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with the instruction through IF/ID\nand ID/EX. Only apply a prediction when the instruction is accepted:\nimem is ready, no load-use/data-memory hold exists, and no older EX\ncorrection squashes it. Prediction itself must not \n…[9054 of 12126 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:68cee13a985e4b7d]…\ntions/s at 48.089 MHz\nand 4,201,740 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md favors retaining\nits direction-only recovery and the retimed divider. The previous tagged\nnext-PC predictor saved only about 0.4% more cycles than direct prediction.\nA fresh architectural trace of the unchanged canonical 2K/10-iteration\nbinary reproduced all five CRCs and the validation message. Inside the\nmarkers it contains 529,588 conditional branches and 135,320 immediately\nload-dependent branches. Sequential trace replay of the current bimodal\npolicy estimates 51,857 total direction misses, only 7,199 on these\nload-dependent branches. A 64x8 local-history/256x2 pattern predictor saved\njust 4,109 direction misses, too little to justify its extra lookup logic.\nThe stronger opportunity is using the existing approximately 94.7% accurate\npredictions on load-dependent branches to avoid their mandatory issue\nbubble. These trace counts are analytical evidence, not a cycle simulation\nor a measurement of the proposed circuit.\n\nNOTES \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
