{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor' and 'r3s2' in d['id']:\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\ncat fpga/bench_stall_gen.sv\"", "aggregated_output": "Add one 64-entry, two-bit bimodal predictor for direct conditional\nbranches and JAL, using the actual fetched instruction's immediate for\nthe target. Couple this predictor to direction-only EX recovery and\nvalid/flush-based squash control. This is one direct-transfer predictor\ndesign: its recovery and squash plumbing must preserve the timing gains\nof the existing divider. Do not add a BTB, JALR predictor, return stack,\nglobal history, extra pipeline stage or arithmetic-unit changes.\n\nIndex the 128-bit counter table with fetch PC[7:2], initialize all\ncounters to weakly not-taken (01), and use the MSB as the conditional\ndirection prediction. Predecode raw imem_data, recognizing only BRANCH\nfunct3 values 0, 1, 4, 5, 6 and 7, or JAL. JAL predicts taken. Form the\nsign-extended B/J immediate and add it to the full 32-bit fetch PC.\nAllow a taken prediction only for word-aligned source and target PCs\nin [0x00000000, 0x00100000); detect range after the full addition, without\ntruncating or wrapping addresses into memory. Otherwise predict PC+4.\nJALR and all non-control or reserved encodings predict fall-through.\nKeep imem_addr driven by the PC register. Select a prediction only when\nthe fetched instruction is accepted into ID/EX: imem ready, no load-use\nhold, no dmem hold, no execute_wait and no older EX correction. Reset\nwins first, then advancing EX correction, then fetch hold, then the\npredicted successor. An advancing correction wins over imem unready.\n\nCarry only a predicted_taken bit with the instruction in IF/ID and\nID/EX, holding it with the instruction on stalls. It records the actual\ngated choice, not merely the direction counter MSB. A direct target is\nalways derived from this exact instruction and PC at fetch and EX;\ntherefore checking the direction suffices without storing or comparing\na 32-bit predicted next PC. Counter aliasing can affect direction but\ncannot supply another instruction's target. Both the PC choice and\ncaptured bit use pre-edge counter state, with no training bypass.\n\nIn EX define actual_take\n…[4125 of 7197 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f2a9f65cc08d93e3]…\npair.\nmodule bench_stall_gen (\n  input  logic clock,\n  input  logic reset,\n  output logic imem_ready,\n  output logic dmem_ready\n);\n\n  function automatic [31:0] xs32(input [31:0] s);\n    reg [31:0] t;\n    begin\n      t = s ^ (s << 13);\n      t = t ^ (t >> 17);\n      xs32 = t ^ (t << 5);\n    end\n  endfunction\n\n  function automatic accept(input [31:0] s);\n    accept = (s[6:0] < 7'd100);\n  endfunction\n\n  // State after the two draws of the current cycle.\n  logic [31:0] state;\n  logic [31:0] next_i, next_d;\n  assign next_i = xs32(state);\n  assign next_d = xs32(next_i);\n\n  // Reset values: state after the two draws of cycle 0, and the accept\n  // bits of those two draws (seed 0xDEADBEEF; see test_bench_stall_gen.py).\n  always_ff @(posedge clock or posedge reset) begin\n    if (reset) begin\n      state      <= 32'h8E1D9142;\n      imem_ready <= 1'b1;\n      dmem_ready <= 1'b1;\n    end else begin\n      state      <= next_d;\n      imem_ready <= accept(next_i);\n      dmem_ready <= accept(next_d);\n    end\n  end\n\nendmodule\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
