d on the\nold combinational-divider baseline at 5.359 MHz, not on the current\n30.789 MHz multicycle-divider champion. Its regression classification\ndoes not show that prediction loses throughput on the champion.\nLESSONS.md identifies removal of combinational division as the major\nclock improvement; preserve that implementation completely. The current\nworst path remains inside the registered divider, leaving a plausible\ntiming budget for a compact predictor. Predicting cached targets also\ncovers repeated JALR targets that the previous B/J-only design missed.\n\nAdd one 64-entry direct-mapped tagged next-PC predictor inside if_s\n…[12515 of 15587 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8385bc5b2a952953]…\ner/s at 48.333 MHz and 4,010,178 timed\nCoreMark cycles. The prior predictor attempt hyp-20261003-003-r4s2\neliminated load-to-branch interlocks through delayed verification,\nreducing its parent's cycles from 4,201,740 to 4,070,414, but its\n47.211 MHz clock did not beat the winning fetch queue. Its worst path\ncrossed ID, the EX ALU and EX control. A fresh architectural trace of\nthe canonical binary confirms 135,320 immediate load-dependent branches\namong 529,588 conditional branches. A marker-local sequential replay of\nthe current 64-counter policy misses 7,198 of those dependent branches;\nonly 13,735 returns leave little headroom for a return predictor. The\ncomplete program's UART contains all five canonical CRCs and the required\nvalidation message. Preserve the champion's fetch queue and try a narrow\nverification sidecar with validity-only recovery. Its late correction\nmust not mux ordinary ID/EX or EX/MEM payloads. The trace and predictor\nreplay do not model stalls, speculative fetches or delayed training.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
