{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category') == 'predictor':\n  print('\\\\n', d['id'],d['title'], {k:d.get(k) for k in ['outcome','fitness','cycles','fmax_mhz']})\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  if isinstance(h,dict):\n   print('MOTIVATION:', h.get('motivation',''))\n   if 'r4s2' in d['id'] or 'r2s2' in d['id']:\n    print('HYPOTHESIS:', h.get('hypothesis',''))\n  print('NOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 Fetch-stage bimodal prediction with immediate-derived targets {'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 12.75, 'cycles': 4201766, 'fmax_mhz': 5.359}\nMOTIVATION: The baseline predicts sequential execution and flushes fetch/decode for\nevery taken conditional branch and every JAL/JALR. Despite the five-stage\ndescription, IF/ID is combinational: each redirect discards one younger\nfetch slot. CoreMark's repeated loops and biased conditional branches\nshould provide enough predictable transfers to reduce measured cycles.\nThere are no predictor attempts in experiments/log.jsonl and no LESSONS.md.\nThe recorded 199.837 ns critical path runs through forwarding and the ALU,\nso this proposal targets iterations/cycle, with approximately unchanged\nFmax. The added predictor must remain small to limit placement disruption.\n\nNOTES: \n\n hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 Tagged next-PC prediction for branches and repeated indirect jumps {'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 77.08, 'cycles': 4186330, 'fmax_mhz': 32.267}\nMOTIVATION: The current core fetches sequentially and discards one younger fetch slot\nwhenever EX resolves a taken branch or jump; IF/ID is combinational, so\nthe penalty is one slot rather than two registered front-end stages.\nThe prior predictor hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 reduced measured CoreMark cycles\nfrom 4,491,485 to 4,201,766, a 6.45% reduction. It was evaluated on the\nold combinational-divider baseline at 5.359 MHz, not on the current\n30.789 MHz multicycle-divider champion. Its regression classification\ndoes not show that prediction loses throughput on the champion.\nLESSONS.md identifies removal of combinational division as the major\nclock improvement; preserve that implementation completely. The current\nworst path remains inside the registered divider, leaving a plausible\ntiming budget for a compact predictor. Predicting cached targets also\ncovers repeated JALR targets that the previous B/J-only design missed.\n\nHYPOTHESIS: Add one 64-entry direct-mapped tagged next-PC predictor inside if_stage.\nEach entry sto\n…[14073 of 17145 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:017074c18f941bff]…\n6s2 Load-to-branch prediction with parallel validation and registered recovery {'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 117.28, 'cycles': 3909350, 'fmax_mhz': 45.847}\nMOTIVATION: The champion's 64-counter predictor and two-entry fetch queue deliver\n120.53 iter/s at 48.333 MHz and 4,010,178 timed CoreMark cycles. The\nr4s2 trace study found 135,320 immediately load-dependent branches out\nof 529,588 conditional branches; improving direction history saved only\n4,109 misses, and return prediction has little dynamic opportunity.\nThe r5s2 predictor instead removed load-to-branch issue bubbles, cutting\ncycles to 3,909,350, but its 48.605 MHz median clock produced only a\n3.15% fitness gain, below the 4.6% acceptance threshold. It verified\nspeculative branches in MEM from WB data, placing a load-value mux and\ncomparator before the late squash controls. Retiming that verification\nbeside the completing load offers a focused way to retain the measured\ncycle benefit while shortening the predictor's recovery feedback.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"The history shows little remaining gain from larger direction tables or return prediction. Load-dependent branch speculation saved about 2.5% of cycles, but its recovery logic hurt timing. I’m checking whether value prediction could cover more load-use stalls while keeping verification separate from the ALU path."}}
